  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(103.46pt, 267.77pt, "As the characteristic dimensions of semiconductor devices continue to approach the nanoscale, heat ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 283.37pt, "transport and thermal energy storage behavior in materials and interfaces exhibit increasingly pronounced ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 298.97pt, "dependencies on length scale, structure, and interfacial properties. Accurate characterization of key ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 314.57pt, "thermophysical properties, including thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and volumetric heat ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 330.17pt, "capacity, is therefore essential for the rational design of functional materials and the thermal management of ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 345.77pt, "advanced electronic devices. Thermoreflectance techniques based on photothermal excitation combine noncontact ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 361.37pt, "operation, high spatial and temporal resolution, and a broad measurement range, and have emerged as powerful ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 376.97pt, "tools for micro- and nanoscale thermophysical characterization. This review systematically surveys the physical ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 392.57pt, "principles and technical characteristics of transient thermoreflectance (TTR), time-domain thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 408.17pt, "(TDTR), frequency-domain thermoreflectance (FDTR), steady-state thermoreflectance (SSTR), spatial-domain ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 423.79pt, "thermoreflectance (SDTR), and the square-pulsed source (SPS) method. Within a unified heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 439.39pt, "framework, these techniques are compared with respect to parameter sensitivity, measurement uncertainty, and ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 454.99pt, "applicable measurement regimes. Representative applications are further used to delineate the conditions under ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 470.59pt, "which thermoreflectance techniques are suitable for characterizing low-thermal-conductivity materials, ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 486.19pt, "anisotropic films, crystalline materials, and multilayer heterostructures, as well as to elucidate the complementary ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 501.79pt, "roles of different methods in quantifying thin-film thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and in-")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 517.39pt, "plane/cross-plane heat transport. Finally, emerging directions are discussed, including ultrahigh spatiotemporal ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 532.99pt, "resolution, multiphysics coupling, in-line industrial inspection, intelligent data processing, and natural-language-")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 548.59pt, "driven analysis, highlighting opportunities to advance both fundamental studies of micro- and nanoscale heat ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 564.19pt, "transport and thermal management technologies for advanced devices. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(53.76pt, 587.62pt, "Keywords  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9.96pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(112.1pt, 587.62pt, "thermoreflectance measurement, micro- and nanoscale heat conduction, thermophysical property ")
  #line-at(53.76pt, 603.22pt, "characterization, interfacial thermal conductance, photothermal techniques, intelligent experimentation ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 644.26pt, "With the rapid development of functional materials and high-performance electronic devices, accurate ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 659.86pt, "characterization of micro- and nanoscale heat transport has become essential for elucidating fundamental heat-transport ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 675.46pt, "mechanisms, optimizing device thermal management, and assessing operational reliability [1, 2]. At the material level, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 691.06pt, "heat transport in low-dimensional materials, anisotropic crystals, polymer films, multilayer heterostructures, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 706.66pt, "thermally functional materials commonly exhibits pronounced dependence on characteristic length scales, interfaces, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 722.26pt, "and microstructure. Transport parameters such as thermal conductivity (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(351.67pt, 722.26pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(357.79pt, 722.26pt, ") and interfacial thermal conductance (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(519.94pt, 722.26pt, "𝐺")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(527.02pt, 722.26pt, ") are ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 737.86pt, "strongly influenced by boundary and interface scattering, crystallographic orientation, and processing conditions; ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 753.48pt, "volumetric heat capacity (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(157.58pt, 753.48pt, "𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(164.3pt, 753.48pt, ") may also vary with composition, phase state, and crystallinity. Consequently, conventional ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 769.08pt, "bulk thermophysical properties often do not adequately represent the actual thermal response of micro- and ")
]

// arXiv source page 2
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.25pt, 803.64pt, "2 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "nanostructures [3-5]. At the device level, advances in high-power and high-frequency electronics, including 5G ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "communications and gallium nitride (GaN) high-electron-mobility transistors (HEMTs), have driven local power ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "densities and heat fluxes to ever higher levels. Channel hot spots, thin-film thermal resistance, and thermal resistance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "at heterogeneous interfaces have consequently become important constraints on device performance and lifetime [6]. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "These effects are particularly pronounced when the characteristic dimensions of a material or device become ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "comparable to phonon mean free paths, such that boundary scattering, interfacial thermal resistance, and anisotropic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "heat diffusion become increasingly significant [7, 8]. Under such conditions, conventional macroscopic measurement ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "techniques and homogeneous-medium assumptions become inadequate, imposing increasingly stringent requirements ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "on the accuracy and spatial resolution of micro- and nanoscale thermophysical-property measurements [1, 9]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 208.7pt, "Compared with macroscopic measurements, experimental determination of micro- and nanoscale thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "properties is not simply a matter of reducing the measurement dimensions; rather, it introduces simultaneous constraints ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "associated with spatial resolution, temporal resolution, and coupled multiparameter inversion. First, temperature and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "heat-flux fields in micro- and nanoscale materials and devices are often highly localized. Hot spots may be concentrated ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "within thin films, heterogeneous interfaces, transistor channels, or microscopic defects, requiring micrometer or even ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "submicrometer spatial resolution to resolve the local thermal response of the region of interest. Second, processes such ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "as phonon relaxation, interfacial energy transfer, and transient heat diffusion in thin films span a broad range of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "timescales, from picoseconds to milliseconds, requiring sufficient temporal resolution and dynamic range. More ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "importantly, the experimental signal depends not only on unknown thermal conductivity, volumetric heat capacity, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "interfacial thermal conductance, but also on structural and experimental parameters such as film thickness and laser-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "spot size. Strongly correlated parameter sensitivities can render the inverse problem ill-posed, amplifying the effects of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "experimental noise, model simplifications, and errors in independently determined input parameters, thereby degrading ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "measurement accuracy and reliability [10, 11]. There is therefore a strong need for micro- and nanoscale thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "measurement techniques that combine high spatiotemporal resolution, noncontact detection, and robust parameter-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "estimation frameworks to support fundamental investigations of heat transport in complex materials and the thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "design of advanced electronic devices. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 458.35pt, "Given these measurement challenges, existing thermophysical characterization methods remain subject to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "substantial limitations at the micro- and nanoscale [12]. Among steady-state methods, the guarded-hot-plate approach ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "is well established and physically intuitive, but its requirements on sample size and geometry make it unsuitable for ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "local thermophysical measurements. Microbridge methods can characterize heat transport in micro- and nanostructures, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "but are susceptible to contact thermal resistance, edge heat losses, and parasitic heat flow through sensor leads [13]. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "The transient plane source (TPS) method is highly reliable for bulk materials, yet its thermal penetration depth is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "coupled to sensor dimensions, and its spatial resolution is typically limited to the millimeter scale, making it difficult ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "to characterize micrometer- or submicrometer-scale films, interfaces, and local hot spots [14]. Moreover, for low-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "dimensional materials, anisotropic films, and multilayer heterostructures, contact-based methods and techniques ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "requiring integrated sensors may struggle to distinguish in-plane and cross-plane heat-transport contributions or to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "resolve interlayer thermal boundary conductance without perturbing the system under study. Precise micro- and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "nanoscale thermophysical measurements therefore require characterization techniques capable of combining localized ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "excitation, noncontact detection, and highly sensitive temperature readout. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 661.18pt, "Against this background, thermoreflectance (TR), a photothermal technique based on optical excitation and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "detection, has emerged as a powerful tool for micro- and nanoscale thermophysical characterization [15]. A pump beam ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "produces a localized temperature rise at the sample surface or within a metallic transducer layer, while a probe beam ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 707.98pt, "detects the small reflectivity change induced by the resulting temperature variation. When interpreted using an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "appropriate heat-diffusion model, the measured thermoreflectance response provides access to heat-transport dynamics ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "and thermophysical properties. By adjusting experimental parameters such as laser-spot size, modulation frequency, or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "pump-probe delay time, different thermoreflectance techniques can achieve micrometer-scale or submicrometer spatial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 770.4pt, "resolution and collectively span a broad temporal range from picoseconds to seconds, making them well suited for ")
]

// arXiv source page 3
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-03.png", width: 492pt), 51pt, 174.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.25pt, 803.64pt, "3 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "characterizing heat transport in thin films, heterogeneous interfaces, multilayer structures, and anisotropic materials. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "After decades of development and methodological refinement [16-18], thermoreflectance has diversified into several ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "distinct approaches (Fig. 1), including transient thermoreflectance (TTR), time-domain thermoreflectance (TDTR), ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "frequency-domain ")
  #line-at(140.666pt, 115.1pt, "thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(230.553pt, 115.1pt, "(FDTR), ")
  #line-at(281.188pt, 115.1pt, "steady-state ")
  #line-at(344.702pt, 115.1pt, "thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(434.589pt, 115.1pt, "(SSTR), ")
  #line-at(483.418pt, 115.1pt, "spatial-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "thermoreflectance (SDTR), and the square-pulsed source (SPS) method. These approaches differ in their excitation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "schemes, signal-acquisition domains, and parameter-inversion strategies and thus offer complementary capabilities ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "across different material systems, spatiotemporal regimes, and thermophysical quantities of interest.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#0000ff"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.33pt, "Figure 1  Development timeline and mainstream branches of thermoreflectance measurement")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 427.15pt, "Modern pump-probe thermoreflectance can be traced back to the 1980s. In 1986, Paddock and Eesley used a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "picosecond pulsed-laser pump-probe system with a mechanical delay stage to quantitatively measure the thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "diffusivity of metal films using transient thermoreflectance, establishing the experimental basis for TTR-based thin-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "film thermophysical characterization [19]. Subsequently, the Maris group conducted a series of studies on picosecond ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "ultrasonics, coherent phonons, and ultrafast pump-probe processes, thereby extending the technique to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "characterization of thin-film acoustics and heat transport phenomena [20-26]. Kading et al. further extended transient ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "photothermal measurements to the characterization of heat transport in dielectric films and multilayer structures [27]. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "Building on these developments, Cahill and co-workers systematically established an axisymmetric heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "model for multilayer structures under Gaussian-beam heating and integrated this framework with femtosecond pump-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "probe optics, pump modulation, and lock-in detection. Together, these advances established the experimental and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "analytical framework for TDTR [28] and enabled increasingly mature quantitative measurements of thin-film thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "conductivity and interfacial thermal conductance [29-37]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 614.38pt, "As research targets expanded from relatively simple single-layer films to low-thermal-conductivity materials, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "anisotropic crystals, and complex multilayer heterostructures, the limitations of relying on a single experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "variable for achieving sufficient parameter sensitivity, extending the measurable range, and decoupling multiple ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "thermophysical parameters became increasingly apparent. These challenges motivated the development of frequency-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "domain, steady-state, and spatial-domain thermoreflectance implementations, as well as strategies that combine ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "information from multiple measurement domains. FDTR varies the pump modulation frequency to tune the thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 707.98pt, "penetration depth and redistribute sensitivity among different thermophysical parameters, thereby providing frequency-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "domain information for parameter identification in multilayer structures [10]. SSTR measures the steady-state ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "temperature rise under continuous or low-frequency heating and enables thermophysical characterization over a broad ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "range of thermal conductivities [38]. Spatially scanned thermoreflectance and beam-offset methods introduce the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 770.4pt, "relative lateral position of the pump and probe beams as an additional measurement variable, thereby enhancing ")
]

// arXiv source page 4
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-04.png", width: 14pt), 268.5pt, 752pt))[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.25pt, 803.64pt, "4 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "sensitivity to in-plane heat diffusion and thermal-conductivity anisotropy [39]. More recently, SPS has employed ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "square-wave modulation and full-period temperature-waveform analysis, utilizing the thermal responses during both ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "the heating and cooling portions of each cycle to introduce an additional measurement dimension for the simultaneous ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "estimation of thermal conductivity, volumetric heat capacity, and related interfacial parameters. Its applications have ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "subsequently been extended to low-thermal-conductivity materials and multilayer heterostructures [40-43]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 146.3pt, "Although thermoreflectance has been widely used for the quantitative characterization of micro- and nanoscale ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "thermophysical properties, most existing reviews and studies focus on a single technique or a specific class of materials ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "[44-46]. A comprehensive comparison of the underlying physical connections, performance limits, and regimes of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "applicability among the different thermoreflectance methods remains limited. Because the various thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "approaches differ markedly in excitation mode, experimental configuration, and signal-acquisition format, they are ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "often treated as independent techniques. At the fundamental level, however, they are governed by the same sequence ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "of physical processes: photothermal excitation generates a temperature field in the sample; the temperature-induced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "reflectivity change provides the thermal signal; and a heat-diffusion model is then used to infer the relevant ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "thermophysical properties. Their principal differences lie in the choice of experimental observable or control variable—")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "such as pump–probe delay time, modulation frequency, steady-state temperature rise, or spatial position—as well as in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "the corresponding signal-processing and parameter-estimation strategies. Without a unified heat-diffusion framework, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "researchers working with complex systems such as multilayer films, heterogeneous interfaces, low-thermal-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "conductivity materials, and anisotropic crystals may find it difficult to identify the most appropriate measurement ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "approach and experimental conditions, or to quantitatively assess parameter sensitivity, inversion stability, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "measurement uncertainty.")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 380.33pt, "Motivated by these considerations, this review begins with the thermoreflectance effect and the governing heat-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "diffusion equations and provides a systematic examination of the physical principles, experimental configurations, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "parameter-sensitivity characteristics of representative thermoreflectance techniques, including TTR, TDTR, FDTR, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "SSTR, SDTR, and SPS. Their common physical basis and distinguishing characteristics are then established within a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "unified heat-diffusion framework. The methods are systematically compared from four complementary perspectives: ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "characteristic heat-diffusion scales, observables and control variables, capabilities for resolving in-plane and cross-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "plane heat transport, and the stability of joint multiparameter inversion. Representative examples involving low-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "thermal-conductivity polymers, anisotropic films, crystalline materials, and GaN/Si heterostructures are used to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "illustrate the capabilities and limitations of different thermoreflectance approaches for characterizing complex materials ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "and devices. Finally, emerging opportunities and challenges are considered, including higher spatiotemporal resolution, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "in situ multiphysics characterization, rapid parameter estimation, industrial in-line nondestructive inspection, natural-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "language-driven intelligent data analysis, and adaptive experimentation, with the broader goal of advancing both ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "fundamental micro- and nanoscale heat-transport studies and thermal-management strategies for next-generation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "microelectronic devices. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.86pt, "1 ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 614.86pt, "Physical Foundations and Theoretical Framework ")
