  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(71.424pt, 162.02pt, " [48-50]. Because thermoreflectance signals are inherently small, lock-in amplification, balanced detection, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "periodic ensemble averaging are commonly used to improve the signal-to-noise ratio. Under optimized detection ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "bandwidth and integration conditions, equivalent temperature resolutions at the millikelvin level or better can be ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "achieved [51]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 224.3pt, "In practice, a metallic film with a thickness ranging from several tens of nanometers to approximately 100 nm is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "commonly deposited on the sample surface to serve as an optical transducer. This metal layer performs two principal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "functions: photothermal excitation and optical thermometry. It absorbs the pump beam and thereby generates a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "controlled, localized heat source, while its relatively strong thermoreflectance response converts the resulting ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "temperature variation into a measurable optical signal. A suitably selected transducer can also reduce complications ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "arising from the intrinsic optical properties of the underlying sample, thereby simplifying interpretation of the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "signal within a multilayer heat-diffusion framework. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 333.53pt, "The transducer material, thickness, and probe wavelength should therefore be selected in a coordinated manner ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "according to the optical and thermal requirements of the experiment. The probe wavelength is generally chosen to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "provide a relatively large thermoreflectance coefficient while maintaining sufficient optical reflectivity and detection ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "sensitivity. Common configurations include a 532 nm probe with an Au transducer and a 785 nm probe with an Al ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "transducer, although the optimal combination depends on the specific material system and experimental configuration. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "The transducer should be sufficiently thick to be effectively optically opaque at the pump and probe wavelengths, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "thereby suppressing parasitic reflections from underlying layers, but sufficiently thin to minimize perturbations arising ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "from its own heat capacity and lateral heat spreading. Consequently, metallic transducers with thicknesses of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "approximately 50–100 nm are widely used, although the optimal thickness should be determined by considering the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "optical absorption depth, transducer/sample interfacial properties, sensitivity to the target thermophysical parameters, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "and experimental signal-to-noise ratio. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 505.15pt, "It is important to note that thermoreflectance measurements employing a metallic transducer do not directly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "measure the surface temperature of the uncoated material. Rather, they probe the temperature response of the transducer, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "which is thermally coupled to the underlying structure. The measured signal therefore represents the combined thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "response of the multilayer system comprising the transducer, the target film, intervening interfaces, and the substrate. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "Although the transducer enhances measurement sensitivity, reproducibility, and applicability across diverse material ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "systems, it also introduces additional thermal mass, lateral heat conduction, and an additional thermal interface. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "Accordingly, quantitative parameter estimation must account for the transducer thickness and thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "properties, together with the thermal conductance of the transducer/sample interface. Uncertainties in these quantities ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "should also be incorporated into sensitivity and uncertainty analyses because they can propagate directly into the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "extracted thermophysical properties. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.9pt, "1.2 Governing Heat-Diffusion Equation")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 700.18pt, "Thermoreflectance measurements commonly use modulated or pulsed laser irradiation to generate localized ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "photothermal heating. For samples coated with a metallic transducer, the pump energy is absorbed primarily by the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "transducer and converted into heat, giving rise to a spatially and temporally varying temperature field near the sample ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "surface. In most micro- and nanoscale thermoreflectance experiments, provided that the relevant thermal length scales ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "remain sufficiently larger than the characteristic nonequilibrium transport lengths of the dominant heat-carrying ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "phonons, heat conduction within the sample can be described using the classical Fourier heat-diffusion model [28]. For ")
