  #line-at(160.82pt, 364.73pt, "). For a multilayer structure consisting of a metallic transducer, the film under test, and a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "substrate, the layer-specific heat-diffusion equations, together with the associated interfacial and boundary conditions, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "define the forward thermal model from which thermoreflectance signals are calculated. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 411.55pt, "The governing equations above also make clear that the measured thermoreflectance response generally depends ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "on multiple coupled quantities rather than on a single thermophysical parameter. In addition to thermal conductivity, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "volumetric heat capacity, and interfacial thermal conductance, the response is influenced by structural and experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "parameters such as film thickness and laser-spot size. The degree to which each parameter affects the measured signal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "depends on the characteristic time, frequency, and spatial scales probed by the experiment. All thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "techniques excite and observe this controlled heat-diffusion process; they differ primarily in the temporal or spatial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "form of the heat input, the measured observable, and the domain in which the signal is acquired and analyzed. Thus, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "the various techniques can be understood within a unified heat-diffusion framework as different experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "projections of the same underlying thermal transport problem in the time, frequency, and spatial domains, or in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "combinations of these domains. ")
