  #line-at(70.584pt, 528.55pt, "A large single-parameter sensitivity, however, does not necessarily mean that the parameter can be independently ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "estimated. In multiparameter problems, the more important issue is whether the signal responses associated with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "different parameters are sufficiently distinct. When the sensitivity curves of two or more parameters exhibit highly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "similar shapes or dependencies in the time, frequency, or spatial domain, their effects on the signal become strongly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "correlated, leading to parameter coupling and an ill-conditioned inverse problem. Under these circumstances, even ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "when the raw experimental signal has a high signal-to-noise ratio, small measurement errors, model discrepancies, or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "uncertainties in prior parameters can be greatly amplified during inversion, increasing the uncertainty in the extracted ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "thermophysical properties. Sensitivity analysis must therefore address two complementary aspects: first, whether the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "target parameter produces a sufficiently large response in the signal—that is, whether it is detectable under the chosen ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "measurement conditions; and second, whether its response can be distinguished from those of other unknown ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "parameters—that is, whether it is independently identifiable. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 700.18pt, "This parameter coupling is closely related to the fact that thermoreflectance signals often depend on combinations ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "of thermophysical properties rather than on individual parameters independently. Under many representative ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "experimental conditions, the measured signal does not respond independently to ")
