#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(295.25pt, 803.64pt, "9 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "resolve multiple strongly correlated parameters simultaneously. The key to thermoreflectance parameter estimation is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "therefore not merely the selection of a numerical fitting algorithm, but also the deliberate design of experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "variables so that different parameters exhibit distinct sensitivity signatures across multiple measurement conditions, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "thereby improving multiparameter identifiability. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 130.7pt, "Sensitivity analysis is therefore a central component of both experimental design and parameter decoupling. By ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "calculating ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(99.864pt, 146.3pt, "𝑆")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(105.02pt, 148.46pt, "𝑥")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(110.06pt, 146.3pt, " as a function of experimental variables such as delay time, modulation frequency, spot size, or spatial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "offset, one can identify regions of high sensitivity to the target parameter and assess the degree of coupling among ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "different parameters. Specifically, sensitivity analysis can be used to: (1) select appropriate time windows, frequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "ranges, or spatial measurement ranges to enhance sensitivity to the parameter of interest; (2) compare the magnitudes ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "and shapes of sensitivity curves to assess multiparameter separability; (3) combine experimental noise and prior-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "parameter uncertainty to estimate the expected uncertainty in the extracted properties; and (4) guide the design of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "experimental conditions and sample structures, for example by optimizing transducer thickness, spot size, modulation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "frequency, and substrate selection, thereby improving inversion stability. In this sense, control of the thermal penetration ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "depth and geometric length scales can be directly mapped onto control of parameter sensitivity: changing modulation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "frequency and spot size modifies the temperature field and heat-flow pathways and thereby redistributes sensitivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "among the relevant thermophysical parameters. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 317.93pt, "After the experimental configuration has been selected and the measurement window optimized for sensitivity and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "parameter separability, the unknown thermophysical properties are generally obtained by fitting the experimental signal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "to a heat-diffusion model. Nonlinear least squares is widely used in thermoreflectance data analysis; algorithms such ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "as Levenberg–Marquardt iteratively update the unknown parameters to minimize the residual between the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "and modeled signals [56]. For problems involving only a few parameters, reasonable initial estimates, and weak ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "parameter correlations, such local optimization methods are computationally efficient and generally robust. When ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "parameter coupling is strong, however, or when the objective function contains multiple local extrema or the admissible ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "parameter range is broad, the inferred solution can become sensitive to the initial guess, experimental noise, and errors ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "in prior parameters. In practice, numerical optimization should therefore be combined with physical constraints, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "sensitivity analysis, and uncertainty quantification to improve both the stability of the inversion and the credibility of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "the extracted parameters. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 489.55pt, "For problems involving a broad parameter space or strong sensitivity of local optimization to the initial guess, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "global search methods such as particle swarm optimization and genetic algorithms, as well as hybrid approaches that ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "combine global and local optimization, have increasingly been applied to thermoreflectance inversion [57]. These ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "methods can explore a wider region of parameter space and reduce the risk of convergence to suboptimal local solutions, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "but generally require substantially more model evaluations, creating a trade-off between computational efficiency and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "optimization robustness. In recent years, machine-learning methods have also been introduced for rapid ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "thermophysical-property estimation and high-throughput thermoreflectance data processing [58-60]. When the training ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "data adequately cover the relevant experimental and thermophysical parameter space, these methods can substantially ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "reduce the computational cost of repeated inversion. Their predictive accuracy and transferability, however, remain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "dependent on the quality and coverage of the training data, the generalization capability of the model, and the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "consistency between the training framework and the underlying heat-transport physics. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 677.26pt, "2 ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 677.26pt, "Classification and Evolution of Thermoreflectance Measurement Techniques ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 700.18pt, "Building on the thermoreflectance effect, heat-diffusion equation, and parameter-sensitivity analysis introduced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "above, this section discusses the classification and technical evolution of representative thermoreflectance methods, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "including TTR, TDTR, FDTR, SSTR, SDTR, and SPS. Although these approaches differ in optical layout, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "photothermal excitation, and signal acquisition, their underlying physical process is the same: laser-induced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "photothermal excitation generates a temperature response in the sample; the temperature-induced reflectivity change is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "measured as a thermal signal; and a heat-diffusion model is used to estimate the relevant thermophysical properties. ")
]

// arXiv source page 10
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-10.png", width: 415pt), 89pt, 116.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "10 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "The principal distinctions among the methods therefore do not lie in the governing heat-conduction equation itself, but ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "in the excitation scheme, observable, and signal-acquisition dimension used to interrogate the same heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "process.  ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 9pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#0000ff"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 769.8pt, "Figure 3  Schematic diagrams of experimental systems for different thermoreflectance techniques")
]

// arXiv source page 11
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "11 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 83.9pt, "Figure 3 summarizes the experimental configurations of several mainstream thermoreflectance methods in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "approximately chronological order of their development. Early TTR extracted heat-diffusion information by recording ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "the transient temperature decay following pulsed-laser heating. TDTR subsequently incorporated pump modulation, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "lock-in detection, and pump–probe delay scanning within an ultrafast optical architecture, substantially improving the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "quantitative characterization of heat transport in thin films and interfaces. FDTR, by contrast, uses modulation-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "frequency scanning as the primary measurement variable, thereby introducing an independent control dimension ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "through which the thermal penetration depth and associated parameter sensitivities can be tuned for the identification ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "of thermophysical properties in multilayer structures. Later approaches, including SSTR, SDTR, and SPS, further ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "expanded the accessible information by measuring quasi-steady temperature rise, spatially resolved thermal fields, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "full-cycle thermal waveforms, respectively. Taken together, these developments have extended thermoreflectance from ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "predominantly ")
  #line-at(122.403pt, 239.9pt, "single-domain ")
  #line-at(193.928pt, 239.9pt, "measurements ")
  #line-at(265.567pt, 239.9pt, "toward ")
  #line-at(306.106pt, 239.9pt, "increasingly ")
  #line-at(368.991pt, 239.9pt, "multidimensional ")
  #line-at(453.909pt, 239.9pt, "and ")
  #line-at(480.541pt, 239.9pt, "complementary ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "characterization strategies. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 271.13pt, "The historical sequence shown in Fig. 3 is not adopted directly in the discussion that follows, because a purely ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "chronological presentation would obscure the more fundamental distinctions among the methods in their characteristic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "heat-diffusion scales, observables, control variables, and parameter sensitivities. We therefore organize the following ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "discussion according to the sequence “quasi-steady response → single-pulse transient response → frequency-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "response → ultrafast pump–probe time-domain response → full-period waveform response → spatial-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "response”, corresponding to SSTR, TTR, FDTR, TDTR, SPS, and SDTR, respectively. SSTR is considered first not ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "because it was developed earliest, but because the steady-state response represents the long-time asymptotic limit of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "diffusive heat transport and thus provides a useful physical baseline for interpreting the non-steady techniques. Building ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "on this framework, we then compare the methods in terms of characteristic heat-diffusion length scales, parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "sensitivity and identifiability, multiparameter estimation capability, and applicable measurement regimes. Figure 4 ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "provides a complementary overview of their approximate measurement ranges and representative application spaces. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.47pt, "2.1 Steady-State Limit: SSTR ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 481.75pt, "Steady-state thermoreflectance (SSTR) [38] generally uses a low-frequency-modulated continuous-wave laser as ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "the pump heat source. When the period of the heating modulation is sufficiently long compared with the characteristic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "thermal relaxation time of the system, the temperature field approaches a quasi-steady state during each heating interval; ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "SSTR can therefore be viewed as the long-time limiting response of the heat-diffusion process. Rather than resolving ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "the transient temperature decay following pulsed excitation, SSTR characterizes the quasi-steady temperature rise ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "generated under a prescribed absorbed power and laser-spot size. The underlying physical picture is straightforward: ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "under otherwise identical heating conditions, a material with higher thermal conductivity transports heat away from the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "heated region more efficiently and therefore exhibits a smaller surface temperature rise, whereas a material with lower ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "thermal conductivity produces stronger local heat accumulation and, consequently, a larger quasi-steady temperature ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "rise. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 637.78pt, "As illustrated in Fig. 3d, a typical SSTR system consists of a low-frequency-modulated continuous-wave pump ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "beam, a continuous-wave probe beam, and a reflected-signal acquisition module [38]. The pump generates localized ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "heating at the sample surface or within a metallic transducer, while the probe detects the resulting temperature-induced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "change in reflectivity. During data analysis, the effective thermal conductivity of the material or the effective thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "resistance of the measured structure can be inferred either by comparing the temperature rise of the sample with that of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "a reference sample under equivalent heating conditions or by interpreting the measured temperature rise using the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "absorbed pump power, laser-spot size, and an appropriate steady-state heat-diffusion model. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 747pt, "SSTR offers an intuitive physical interpretation and a relatively simple experimental configuration. Because the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "steady-state temperature field is governed by the balance between heat input and heat conduction and therefore does ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "not explicitly depend on volumetric heat capacity, the method is well suited to characterizing the effective thermal ")
]

// arXiv source page 12
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "12 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "conductivity of bulk or relatively thick materials, as well as the effective thermal resistance of layered structures. For ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "multilayer films, buried interfaces, or heterostructures with complex heat-flow pathways, however, the quasi-steady ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "temperature rise generally contains coupled contributions from the thermal resistances of individual layers and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "interfaces together with substrate heat spreading. A single steady-state temperature-rise amplitude is therefore often ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "insufficient to independently resolve the thermophysical properties of multiple constituent layers and interfaces. This ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "limitation reflects the reduced dimensionality of the steady-state observable rather than insufficient signal sensitivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "alone. When layer- or interface-specific characterization, multiparameter decoupling, or time-dependent heat-transport ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "information is required, additional observables in the time, frequency, or spatial domains are needed. A natural departure ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "from this long-time limit is to resolve the temperature evolution following a discrete heating event, which leads to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "transient thermoreflectance (TTR) approach discussed next.")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 240.02pt, "2.2 Single-Pulse Transient Response: TTR ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 263.33pt, "Transient thermoreflectance (TTR) [61] uses a pulsed laser to generate an impulsive or short-duration thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 278.93pt, "excitation at the sample surface and records the subsequent temporal decay of the surface temperature, making it one ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 294.53pt, "of the most direct time-domain thermoreflectance methods. Whereas SSTR primarily uses the quasi-steady ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "temperature-rise amplitude, the thermophysical information in TTR is encoded mainly in the characteristic timescale ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 325.73pt, "and temporal shape of the transient temperature response: faster heat diffusion produces a more rapid decay of the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "surface temperature, whereas slower heat diffusion leads to a more persistent thermal response. The transient decay ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "curve therefore provides time-resolved information on thermal diffusion and associated thermophysical properties. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 372.53pt, "As shown in Fig. 3a, TTR commonly uses a nanosecond or picosecond pulsed laser as the pump [27, 62] to induce ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "a rapid temperature rise at the sample surface or within a metal transducer, while a continuous-wave probe monitors ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "the heated region. A high-speed photodetector records the temperature-induced change in probe reflectivity, yielding ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "the temporal evolution of the surface thermal response. During data analysis, the transient temperature-rise signal is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "often normalized to its peak value to reduce sensitivity to multiplicative amplitude factors, such as absorbed pump ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "energy, the thermoreflectance coefficient, and system gain. Parameter extraction can then rely more strongly on the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "characteristic decay timescale and waveform shape rather than on the absolute signal amplitude. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 481.75pt, "TTR provides a clear physical picture and directly resolves the temporal relaxation of a thermal disturbance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "following pulsed excitation. It is particularly suitable for relatively thick films, bulk materials, and samples whose ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "characteristic thermal relaxation times lie within the accessible temporal bandwidth of the measurement system. In ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "multilayer heterostructures, low-thermal-conductivity materials, or systems with appreciable interfacial thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "resistance, however, the transient decay curve is generally influenced simultaneously by film thermal conductivity, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "volumetric heat capacity, interfacial thermal conductance, and substrate properties. The corresponding sensitivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "signatures may overlap substantially within a single temporal window, leading to strong parameter correlations and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "limiting independent multiparameter estimation from a single decay trace. In addition, detector bandwidth and signal-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "to-noise ratio constrain the experimentally accessible time window, while the progressive decrease in temperature-rise ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "amplitude at long delay times makes the signal increasingly susceptible to experimental noise. These factors can restrict ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "the characterization of slow thermal relaxation and long-range heat diffusion. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 653.38pt, "Overall, TTR establishes a fundamental time-domain measurement framework based on the relaxation of an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "impulsively generated thermal disturbance, but a single transient response provides limited active control over the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "characteristic heat-diffusion length and the distribution of parameter sensitivities. Improved parameter identifiability ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "therefore requires additional experimental degrees of freedom that systematically alter the sampled heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "regime. One such variable is modulation frequency, which continuously tunes the thermal penetration depth and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "redistributes sensitivity among film, interface, and substrate properties. This concept forms the basis of the frequency-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "domain thermoreflectance (FDTR) method discussed next. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.32pt, "2.3 Frequency-Domain Response: FDTR ")
]

// arXiv source page 13
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "13 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 68.28pt, "Frequency-domain thermoreflectance (FDTR) periodically modulates the pump intensity to generate a periodic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "thermal response in the sample and measures the amplitude and phase of the thermoreflectance signal relative to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "modulated heat source over a range of modulation frequencies. Unlike TTR, which directly records transient ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "temperature decay after a pulse, FDTR uses modulation frequency as the primary measurement variable and probes the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "complex thermal response in the frequency domain. A central feature of FDTR is the ability to tune the characteristic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "thermal penetration depth by varying the modulation frequency: at higher frequencies, the thermal disturbance is more ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "strongly confined to the near-surface region, whereas at lower frequencies, the penetration depth increases, allowing ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "deeper portions of the multilayer structure, including buried interfaces and the substrate, to contribute more strongly to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "the measured response. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 208.7pt, "As shown in Fig. 3c, a typical FDTR system uses continuous-wave pump and probe beams. The pump is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "periodically modulated using an electro-optic modulator, an acousto-optic modulator, or direct intensity modulation, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "while the probe is focused on the heated region to detect the temperature-induced oscillation in reflectivity. A lock-in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "amplifier extracts the amplitude and phase of the thermoreflectance response at the pump modulation frequency [10, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "63]. Scanning the modulation frequency over a selected range yields a complex frequency-dependent thermal response, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "which can be interpreted using a multilayer heat-diffusion model to estimate parameters such as film thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, substrate thermophysical properties, and the areal heat capacity of the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "transducer. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 333.53pt, "Physically, different modulation frequencies correspond to different characteristic thermal penetration depths and, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "consequently, different distributions of parameter sensitivity within the multilayer structure. Frequency scanning ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "therefore allows FDTR to vary the relative contributions of different layers and interfaces, providing a degree of thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "depth discrimination. At relatively high modulation frequencies, the thermal disturbance is concentrated near the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "surface, and the signal tends to exhibit greater sensitivity to the transducer and near-surface thermal properties. As the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "modulation frequency decreases and the thermal penetration depth increases, buried layers, interfaces, and the substrate ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "increasingly influence the measured response. This behavior should not, however, be interpreted as a sharply defined ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "depth resolution or strict spatial isolation of individual layers; rather, frequency variation continuously redistributes the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "sensitivity weights associated with different thermophysical parameters and regions of the multilayer stack. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "Accordingly, there is no universal boundary separating high-, intermediate-, and low-frequency sensitivity regimes, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "because the relevant frequency range depends jointly on thermal diffusivity, layer thicknesses, laser-spot size, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "interfacial thermal conductance, and the overall multilayer architecture. FDTR experiments should therefore employ ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "sensitivity analysis to identify a modulation-frequency range that provides sufficient sensitivity to the target parameters ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "while improving their separability from other unknown quantities. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 551.95pt, "FDTR offers a relatively simple optical configuration and a readily adjustable frequency-domain control variable, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "providing multifrequency thermal-response information without requiring a mechanical delay line. It is therefore well ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "suited to characterizing heat transport in films, interfaces, and multilayer structures. By varying modulation frequency, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "the characteristic heat-diffusion scale can be tuned without altering the sample geometry, providing substantial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "flexibility for experimental design and redistribution of parameter sensitivity. Nevertheless, FDTR remains subject to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "the fundamental challenge of parameter correlation: if multiple thermophysical properties exhibit similar sensitivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "signatures over the selected frequency range, their joint estimation in a complex multilayer system can remain ill-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "conditioned. Low-frequency measurements can also be more susceptible to environmental drift and low-frequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "noise, whereas high-frequency measurements impose increasingly stringent requirements on modulation and detection ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "bandwidth, phase calibration, and signal-to-noise ratio. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 707.98pt, "Overall, FDTR extends thermoreflectance characterization into the frequency domain, with modulation frequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "serving as the primary scan variable for tuning the characteristic heat-diffusion scale and redistributing parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "sensitivity. Frequency scanning alone, however, provides only one experimental dimension for interrogating the thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "response. Introducing pump–probe delay time provides an additional temporal coordinate through which different ")
]

// arXiv source page 14
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "14 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "stages of heat diffusion can be resolved. The simultaneous use of modulation frequency and delay time is a defining ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "feature of time-domain thermoreflectance (TDTR), discussed next. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.22pt, "2.4 Ultrafast Pump-Probe Time-Domain Response: TDTR ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 138.5pt, "Time-domain thermoreflectance (TDTR) is a representative ultrafast thermoreflectance technique based on a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 154.1pt, "pump–probe architecture. Unlike TTR, which directly records the continuous temperature decay following a single ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 169.7pt, "heating pulse, TDTR typically uses a high-repetition-rate femtosecond laser to generate pump and probe pulse trains, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 185.3pt, "periodically modulates the pump intensity, and scans the relative arrival time of the pump and probe pulses using a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 200.9pt, "mechanical delay line to measure the thermoreflectance response as a function of pump–probe delay. Periodic pump ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 216.5pt, "modulation enables phase-sensitive lock-in detection of the weak thermoreflectance signal while also influencing the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 232.1pt, "periodic thermal response and characteristic heat-diffusion scale. Thus, modulation frequency provides an important ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 247.73pt, "experimental control parameter, whereas pump–probe delay time serves as the primary measurement coordinate for ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 263.33pt, "resolving and fitting the transient thermal response. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 278.93pt, "As shown in Fig. 3b, a typical TDTR system consists of a femtosecond pulsed laser, a pump-modulation module, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 294.53pt, "a mechanical delay stage, a pump-probe optical path, and a lock-in detection module [64]. The high-repetition-rate laser ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "pulses are split into pump and probe beams. The pump is intensity-modulated, typically in the MHz range using an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 325.73pt, "electro-optic modulator or similar device, and focused onto the sample surface to produce a train of heating pulses with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "a periodically modulated envelope in the metal transducer. The probe passes through the mechanical delay line, which ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "varies its optical path length and hence its arrival time relative to the pump, and is focused onto the pump-heated region ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "to sample the temperature-dependent reflectivity at different delay times. The lock-in amplifier extracts the in-phase ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "(")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(53.04pt, 388.13pt, "𝑉ᵢₙ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(66.264pt, 388.13pt, ") and out-of-phase (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(148.82pt, 388.13pt, "𝑉ₒᵤₜ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(166.7pt, 388.13pt, ") components at the modulation frequency, and their ratio ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(410.35pt, 388.13pt, "−𝑉ᵢₙ/𝑉ₒᵤₜ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(454.42pt, 388.13pt, " is commonly used as ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "the fitting signal [28]. This ratio reduces sensitivity to multiplicative amplitude factors, including the thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "coefficient, optical gain, and laser power, thereby improving the robustness of quantitative parameter extraction. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 434.95pt, "From a physical perspective, the TDTR signal represents the periodically modulated thermal response generated ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "by a high-repetition-rate sequence of ultrafast heating pulses. Scanning the pump–probe delay samples different stages ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "of the transient thermal evolution on picosecond-to-nanosecond timescales, such that different delay-time ranges can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "exhibit distinct sensitivities to transducer properties, film thermal transport, interfacial thermal conductance, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "substrate properties. At the same time, the pump modulation frequency sets an additional periodic timescale and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "modifies the characteristic thermal penetration depth and heat accumulation, thereby redistributing the relative ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "contributions of the film, interfaces, and substrate to the measured response. Joint selection of the delay-time window ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "and modulation frequency can therefore reshape the sensitivity functions of different parameters, improve their ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "separability, and enhance inversion stability. TDTR thus combines delay-resolved transient information with frequency-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "dependent control of the periodically driven thermal field, providing complementary dimensions for experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "design and sensitivity optimization. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 606.58pt, "With ultrafast pump–probe timing, well-established multilayer heat-diffusion models, and sensitive lock-in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "detection, TDTR has become a widely used method for measuring thin-film thermal conductivity and interfacial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "thermal conductance, particularly in multilayer structures consisting of a metallic transducer, functional film, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "substrate. For nanometer- to micrometer-scale films, the delay-dependent response can be interpreted using a multilayer ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "heat-diffusion model to extract quantities such as cross-plane thermal conductivity and interfacial thermal conductance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "[65]. However, TDTR signals may still respond simultaneously to multiple thermophysical and structural parameters, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "and several unknowns generally cannot be determined independently from a single experimental condition when their ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "sensitivities are highly correlated. In practice, some quantities - such as volumetric heat capacity, film thickness, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "transducer properties, or selected interface parameters - are therefore commonly prescribed or measured independently ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "to reduce parameter correlations and improve inversion stability [46]. The usable ranges of modulation frequency and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "delay time are also constrained by laser repetition rate, pulse heat accumulation, modulator bandwidth, and system ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "signal-to-noise ratio. ")
]

