#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "15 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 68.28pt, "Overall, TDTR uses pump–probe delay time as its principal measurement coordinate while exploiting periodic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "modulation for phase-sensitive detection and additional control of the thermal response. It has become a mature tool ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "for characterizing film thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and heat transport in multilayer structures. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "Its parameter sensitivity can be further tailored through modulation frequency, laser-spot size, and the selected delay-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "time fitting window, providing considerable flexibility for experimental optimization. Nevertheless, for low-thermal-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "conductivity materials, deeply buried interfaces, or systems involving strongly correlated unknown parameters, reliable ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "multiparameter inversion may still require independent prior information, multiple measurement conditions, or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "additional observables. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.82pt, "2.5 Full-Period Waveform Response: SPS ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 232.1pt, "The square-pulsed source (SPS) method [40] is a thermoreflectance technique based on periodic square-wave ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 247.73pt, "photothermal excitation and measurement of the complete temperature response over each modulation period. Unlike ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 263.33pt, "TDTR, which primarily resolves ultrafast thermal dynamics through pump–probe delay scanning, or FDTR, which ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 278.93pt, "measures the amplitude and phase of the periodic response as a function of modulation frequency, SPS uses a square-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 294.53pt, "wave-modulated continuous-wave pump to periodically heat the sample while recording the time-resolved temperature ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "response throughout each heating–cooling cycle. SPS therefore not only tunes the characteristic heat-diffusion scale ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 325.73pt, "through modulation frequency but also retains the intra-period temporal evolution associated with heating and cooling. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "The resulting data provide thermophysical constraints through both frequency-dependent changes in the thermal field ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "and the detailed shape of the full-period temperature waveform. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 372.53pt, "As illustrated in Fig. 3d, the SPS optical configuration resembles that of SSTR in that both can employ continuous-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "wave pump–probe optics, but the two methods differ substantially in excitation protocol, signal-acquisition strategy, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "and information content. SSTR primarily uses the quasi-steady temperature rise obtained under low-frequency heating ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "to infer effective thermal conductivity or effective thermal resistance. SPS instead applies periodic square-wave ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "excitation and records the time-dependent temperature response throughout each modulation period, typically over ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "multiple modulation frequencies. At each frequency, SPS therefore yields not merely a single temperature-rise ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "amplitude or a pair of amplitude and phase values, but a resolved heating–cooling waveform that contains information ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "on the evolution of the thermal field within the modulation cycle. Reported SPS systems span modulation frequencies ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "of approximately 1 Hz to 20 MHz [40], although the experimentally accessible range is ultimately constrained by the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "modulation, detection, and data-acquisition bandwidths, as well as by the required signal-to-noise ratio. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 528.55pt, "Variation of modulation frequency provides SPS with control over the characteristic heat-diffusion length scale. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "According to the thermal-penetration-depth relation discussed above, higher modulation frequencies correspond to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "shorter characteristic diffusion lengths and weight the thermal response more strongly toward the transducer and near-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "surface region. As the modulation frequency decreases, the thermal penetration depth increases, and buried films, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "interfaces, and the substrate can contribute more substantially to the measured response. At sufficiently low frequencies, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "the response approaches the quasi-steady limit and increasingly reflects the combined thermal resistance and heat-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "spreading characteristics of the multilayer system. Thus, varying modulation frequency continuously redistributes the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "relative contributions of different regions and heat-transport processes within the same experimental configuration. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "Importantly, however, individual frequencies do not map uniquely onto specific material layers or thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "properties. As in FDTR, frequency-dependent depth discrimination arises from a continuous redistribution of parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "sensitivities rather than from sharply defined spatial selectivity. The relevant sensitivity ranges remain jointly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "determined by thermal diffusivity, layer thicknesses, laser-spot size, interfacial thermal conductance, and multilayer ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "geometry, and appropriate frequency ranges should therefore be selected using sensitivity analysis. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 731.38pt, "In addition to frequency-dependent control of the heat-diffusion scale, SPS retains the intra-period evolution of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "the temperature response during the heating and cooling portions of each cycle. Different portions of this waveform ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "can exhibit distinct sensitivity signatures for the transducer, constituent layers, interfaces, and substrate, thereby ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "providing additional information for parameter estimation. Compared with reducing the response at each frequency to ")
]

