#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "16 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "a single amplitude or phase quantity, analysis of the full-period waveform can, under suitable experimental conditions, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "increase the number and diversity of useful sensitivity signatures and thereby improve the identifiability of selected ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "thermophysical parameters. This capability can be particularly valuable for low-thermal-conductivity materials and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "multilayer systems in which several thermophysical parameters contribute simultaneously to the measured response. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 130.7pt, "Although SPS expands the accessible frequency-dependent heat-diffusion scales while retaining intra-period ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "temporal information, its measurement capability remains constrained by experimental bandwidth, signal quality, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "parameter coupling. At low modulation frequencies, measurements can become increasingly susceptible to ambient-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "temperature drift, long-term laser-power fluctuations, and low-frequency noise. At high frequencies, the finite rise and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "fall times of the modulator and the bandwidths of the detector and data-acquisition system can distort both the applied ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "heat-source waveform and the measured thermal response, causing the experimental excitation to deviate from an ideal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "square wave. Such instrumental transfer characteristics should therefore be accounted for when they become ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "comparable to the relevant modulation timescales. Moreover, multifrequency full-period measurements provide ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "additional inversion information but do not by themselves guarantee unique multiparameter estimation. If several ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "unknown parameters retain similar sensitivity signatures across the selected frequency and intra-period time ranges, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "reliable extraction still requires appropriate prior constraints, sensitivity-based experimental design, and rigorous ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "uncertainty analysis. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 317.93pt, "Overall, SPS combines broadband square-wave modulation with full-period temperature-waveform acquisition, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "integrating frequency-dependent control of the characteristic heat-diffusion scale with time-resolved information within ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "each modulation cycle. This combination can provide richer sensitivity information for multiparameter estimation than ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "measurements based solely on a single response metric at each frequency. Compared with TDTR, SPS emphasizes ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "periodic thermal evolution over timescales set by the square-wave modulation and detection bandwidth, whereas TDTR ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "provides ultrafast pump–probe access to picosecond-to-nanosecond thermal dynamics; the two methods therefore probe ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "complementary temporal regimes and offer different routes for tailoring parameter sensitivity. SSTR, TTR, FDTR, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "TDTR, and SPS primarily interrogate the thermal response through steady-state, temporal, or frequency-related ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "observables and control variables. An additional and fundamentally different source of information is obtained by ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "resolving the spatial distribution of the temperature field, particularly when in-plane transport or thermal-conductivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "anisotropy is of interest. This motivates the spatial-domain thermoreflectance approaches discussed next. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.27pt, "2.6 Spatial-Domain Response: SDTR and Beam-Offset Methods ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 528.55pt, "Spatial-domain thermoreflectance (SDTR) [39] systematically varies the lateral position of the probe relative to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "the pump and measures the thermoreflectance response as a function of spatial offset, thereby resolving the lateral ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "spreading of the temperature field. Unlike the methods discussed above, which primarily interrogate the thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "response through steady-state, temporal, frequency-dependent, or full-period waveform information, SDTR introduces ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "lateral position as an independent measurement coordinate, making the spatial evolution of in-plane heat diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "directly accessible to parameter estimation. Because the lateral temperature field is particularly sensitive to in-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "heat transport, this approach is well suited to characterizing anisotropic crystals, oriented films, and other material ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "systems exhibiting pronounced directional heat transport. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 653.38pt, "In a typical SDTR experiment, a modulated pump generates a localized periodic heat source at the sample surface, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "while the probe is scanned laterally relative to the pump center and the thermoreflectance response is recorded at each ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "offset. As the probe moves away from the heated region, the amplitude of the temperature oscillation generally ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "decreases, while its phase lag relative to the periodic heat source evolves as heat diffuses laterally through the sample. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "Analysis of the spatial dependence of amplitude or phase, together with an appropriate heat-diffusion model, enables ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "estimation of in-plane thermal conductivity or diffusivity. To enhance sensitivity to lateral heat diffusion, the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "modulation frequency, laser-spot size, and spatial scan range should be jointly selected so that the characteristic lateral ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "diffusion length is comparable to the relevant optical and geometric length scales. If the thermal-diffusion length is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "much smaller than the spot size, the measured spatial profile is dominated more strongly by the optical heating and ")
]

