  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "17 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "probing profiles, reducing sensitivity to intrinsic in-plane transport. Reducing the modulation frequency can increase ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "the lateral diffusion length and broaden the temperature field, thereby enhancing sensitivity to in-plane thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "properties, although this change simultaneously modifies cross-plane heat penetration. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 115.1pt, "Beam-offset TDTR (BO-TDTR) [66, 67] provides a representative example of incorporating spatial scanning into ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "an ultrafast TDTR platform. A pump–probe delay time with high sensitivity to in-plane heat transport is typically ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "selected, after which the lateral offset between the pump and probe is systematically varied to map the spatial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "dependence of the lock-in response. The in-plane thermal conductivity or diffusivity can be inferred from the width ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "and shape of the resulting spatial profile, for example from the full width at half maximum of the out-of-phase ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "component (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(103.1pt, 193.1pt, "𝑉")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(108.26pt, 195.26pt, "out")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(120.86pt, 193.1pt, "), in conjunction with an anisotropic heat-diffusion model. Whereas SDTR commonly analyzes the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "spatial decay and phase evolution of a periodically driven thermal field, BO-TDTR combines pump–probe timing with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "lateral scanning and often extracts in-plane transport information from the spatial broadening of a selected lock-in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "signal. Despite differences in excitation, optical configuration, and signal processing, both approaches treat lateral ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "pump–probe displacement as a measurement coordinate and exploit the resulting spatial dependence to enhance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "sensitivity to in-plane heat transport. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 286.73pt, "Introducing a spatial measurement dimension is particularly valuable for characterizing anisotropic heat transport. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "For crystals or oriented films with strongly direction-dependent thermal conductivity, spatial scans can be performed ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "along different in-plane directions to resolve directional variations in thermal spreading. Combined with an anisotropic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "heat-diffusion model, these measurements can provide direction-dependent in-plane thermal conductivities and, when ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "sufficient angular information and appropriate model constraints are available, enable reconstruction of the principal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "directions and components of the in-plane thermal-conductivity tensor. Relative to measurements relying primarily on ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "temporal or frequency variation, spatial scanning provides an observable directly associated with lateral redistribution ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "of the temperature field and can substantially alter the relative sensitivities to in-plane conductivity, cross-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and other parameters. Spatial information can therefore improve ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "parameter identifiability when its sensitivity signature is sufficiently distinct from those provided by other measurement ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "dimensions. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 458.35pt, "The performance of spatial-domain methods is nevertheless governed by the interplay among thermal-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "length scales, optical-spot dimensions, and multilayer geometry. Increasing lateral thermal spreading generally requires ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "reducing the modulation frequency, but this simultaneously increases the cross-plane thermal penetration depth, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "enhancing sensitivity to the substrate, buried interfaces, and deeper layers and potentially complicating isolation of in-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "plane transport within the target film. Low-frequency measurements are also more susceptible to environmental-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "temperature drift, laser-power fluctuations, and low-frequency noise. Conversely, if the modulation frequency is too ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "high and the lateral diffusion length becomes small relative to the spot size, the measured spatial profile is increasingly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "governed by the convolution of the pump and probe intensity distributions and localized cross-plane heat flow, thereby ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "reducing sensitivity to intrinsic in-plane thermophysical properties [39]. Spatial-domain experiments therefore require ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "coordinated selection of modulation frequency, pump and probe spot sizes, and scan range, guided by characteristic-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "length-scale considerations and quantitative sensitivity analysis. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 629.98pt, "It is also important to distinguish the spatial-domain methods defined here from other thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "approaches that incorporate a nonzero pump–probe offset without using spatial position as the primary measurement ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "coordinate. To avoid ambiguity in classifying beam-offset techniques, we adopt the criterion of whether lateral offset ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "is systematically varied and explicitly enters the parameter inversion as an independent measurement variable. When ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "pump–probe offset is scanned and in-plane heat-transport properties are inferred from the resulting spatial variation in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 707.98pt, "amplitude, phase, or another thermal observable, the method is classified here as spatial-domain thermoreflectance. In ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "contrast, when a lateral offset is fixed and used primarily to modify the heat-flow geometry or redistribute parameter ")
