#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "18 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "the presence of a pump–probe offset itself, but whether lateral position is systematically scanned and constitutes an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "explicit measurement and inversion dimension. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 99.5pt, "Overall, SDTR and related spatial-scanning methods extend thermoreflectance characterization by resolving the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "lateral structure of the temperature field and introducing spatial position as an additional measurement coordinate. This ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "capability provides a particularly direct route to characterizing in-plane thermal conductivity and direction-dependent ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "heat transport. Nevertheless, the measured spatial response remains jointly influenced by cross-plane heat diffusion, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "interfacial thermal conductance, substrate properties, and optical-beam geometry; introducing a spatial observable ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "therefore redistributes and potentially improves parameter sensitivity but does not inherently eliminate parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "correlations. For complex multilayer structures and multiparameter inverse problems, spatial-domain measurements ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "should therefore be designed using sensitivity and uncertainty analyses and, where appropriate, combined with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "independent prior information or complementary temporal, frequency-domain, and full-period measurements such as ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "TDTR, FDTR, and SPS. Such combinations provide a pathway toward multidimensional thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "characterization in which complementary observables are used to improve parameter identifiability across increasingly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "complex material systems. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.45pt, "2.7 Section Summary ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 325.73pt, "In summary, all thermoreflectance methods described above are built on the same thermoreflectance transduction ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "mechanism and underlying heat-diffusion framework, while differing primarily in heat-source excitation, measurement ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "coordinate, thermal observable, and signal-acquisition strategy. SSTR characterizes the quasi-steady temperature rise; ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "TTR records the transient thermal relaxation following pulsed excitation; FDTR varies modulation frequency and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "measures the corresponding amplitude and phase response; TDTR uses pump–probe delay time as its principal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "measurement coordinate while employing periodic modulation for lock-in detection and additional sensitivity control; ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "SPS records the full-period temperature waveform under periodic square-wave excitation, with modulation frequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "providing an additional means of tuning the heat-diffusion scale; and SDTR and related spatial-scanning methods ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "resolve the thermal response as a function of lateral position to probe in-plane heat diffusion. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 466.15pt, "These techniques should therefore not be viewed as a simple chronological progression in which one method ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "supersedes another, but rather as complementary measurement paradigms that interrogate the same heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "problem through quasi-steady, transient, frequency-dependent, ultrafast delay-resolved, full-period waveform, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "spatial responses. This framework classifies each technique according to its primary measurement coordinate and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "observable while recognizing that experimental control variables—including modulation frequency, pump–probe delay, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "laser-spot size, and beam offset—can be combined or varied across different implementations to redistribute parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "sensitivity. The following section therefore compares these methods in terms of characteristic heat-diffusion scales, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "parameter sensitivity and identifiability, capabilities for resolving in-plane and cross-plane transport, practical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "experimental constraints, and representative application regimes. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.66pt, "3 ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 622.66pt, "Measurement Ranges and Applicable Conditions of Different Thermoreflectance Methods ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 645.58pt, "The preceding section introduced representative thermoreflectance methods—SSTR, TTR, FDTR, TDTR, SPS, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "and SDTR—from the perspective of their primary measurement coordinates and observables. Although these ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "techniques share the same thermoreflectance transduction mechanism and underlying heat-diffusion physics, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "differences in excitation, observable, and experimental conditions lead to distinct characteristic heat-diffusion scales, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 707.98pt, "parameter-sensitivity distributions, capabilities for resolving in-plane and cross-plane transport, and suitable ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "application regimes. Rather than discussing each technique individually again, this section provides a comparative ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "assessment based on several considerations central to experimental design: the effective heat-diffusion scale and its ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "experimental control, sensitivity and identifiability of thermophysical parameters, characterization of anisotropic heat ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 770.4pt, "transport, and method selection for different material systems. These comparisons establish a practical framework for ")
]

