#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-19.png", width: 49pt), 248.5pt, 160pt))[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "19 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "matching measurement conditions and observables to the characteristic dimensions and target properties of a given ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "sample and provide a basis for interpreting the representative applications discussed later. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.22pt, "3.1 Effective Heat-Diffusion Scale and Experimental Control ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 138.5pt, "The effective probing range of a thermoreflectance measurement is fundamentally constrained by the characteristic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 154.1pt, "length scales over which heat diffuses during the relevant experimental timescale. For a periodically modulated heat ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 170.06pt, "source, the thermal penetration depth ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(216.05pt, 170.06pt, "𝑑")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(222.17pt, 172.22pt, "𝑝")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(230.33pt, 170.06pt, "= √𝛼/𝜋𝑓")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(272.93pt, 170.06pt, " introduced above can be used to characterize the propagation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 185.3pt, "length of the periodic thermal disturbance, where ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(261.53pt, 185.3pt, "𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(268.13pt, 185.3pt, " is the material thermal diffusivity and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(435.58pt, 185.3pt, "𝑓")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(441.7pt, 185.3pt, " is the pump modulation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 200.9pt, "frequency. For a single-pulse transient process, ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(253.73pt, 200.9pt, "𝑑")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(259.85pt, 203.06pt, "𝑝")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(268.01pt, 200.9pt, "= √𝛼𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(296.93pt, 200.9pt, " can be used to estimate the characteristic diffusion length ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 216.5pt, "over an observation time ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(158.66pt, 216.5pt, "𝜏")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(163.7pt, 216.5pt, ". These characteristic scales should not be interpreted as sharp measurement boundaries or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 232.1pt, "uniquely defined probing depths. Their practical significance lies in comparison with geometric scales such as film ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 247.73pt, "thickness, interface depth, and spot size. The ratios among these thermal and geometric scales determine the dominant ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 263.33pt, "heat-flow regime and strongly influence the sensitivity of the measured signal to different regions and thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 278.93pt, "parameters. Modulation frequency, delay time, pulse-repetition period, spot size, and signal-acquisition window can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 294.53pt, "therefore alter the effective region sampled by the thermal field and redistribute the relative sensitivities to surface ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "layers, buried interfaces, and the substrate. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 325.73pt, "For quasi-steady or long-timescale measurements, heat can spread over comparatively large distances before the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "measured response is established. SSTR uses quasi-steady temperature-rise information and is therefore governed by ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "the combined thermal resistance and heat-spreading behavior of the sample–substrate system. It is particularly useful ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "when the desired quantity is an effective thermal conductivity or equivalent thermal resistance and independent layer-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "by-layer resolution is not required. When films, interfaces, and the substrate all contribute substantially to the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "temperature rise, however, a single quasi-steady observable generally cannot distinguish their individual contributions. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "TTR instead uses the transient temperature decay following pulsed excitation, with the effective diffusion scale ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "determined by the thermal diffusivity and the portion of the transient captured within the accessible observation window. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "It is therefore suitable for relatively thick films, bulk materials, and systems whose characteristic thermal relaxation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "times fall within the effective temporal range of the instrument. For ultrathin films or interface-dominated structures, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "very rapid thermal relaxation can approach the temporal-resolution or detection-bandwidth limits, whereas slowly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "decaying long-time signals can become increasingly noise-limited. In either regime, separation of different ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "thermophysical contributions becomes more difficult, and inversion uncertainty can increase [62, 70]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 528.55pt, "FDTR provides direct experimental control over the characteristic periodic heat-diffusion length by varying the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "modulation frequency. Increasing the modulation frequency reduces the thermal penetration depth and weights the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "response more strongly toward the near-surface region, whereas decreasing the frequency increases the penetration ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "depth and enhances contributions from buried layers, interfaces, and the substrate. Frequency scanning therefore ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "changes both the effective heat-diffusion scale and the distribution of parameter sensitivities throughout the multilayer ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "structure. This tunability does not, however, imply that individual frequencies correspond uniquely to particular depths ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "or layers. In multilayer systems, the sensitivity ranges associated with different layers and interfaces can overlap ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "substantially, such that simply extending the frequency range does not necessarily enable independent multiparameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "estimation. The useful frequency window must instead be determined by considering the sample geometry together ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "with quantitative sensitivity and parameter-correlation analyses [68]. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 684.58pt, "TDTR resolves the thermal response primarily through pump–probe delay scanning, thereby sampling different ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "stages of heat diffusion on picosecond-to-nanosecond timescales. The delay-time window determines which portions ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "of the transient response contribute to fitting, while pump modulation frequency modifies the periodic thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "penetration scale and interpulse heat accumulation and can therefore be used to redistribute parameter sensitivity. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "Measurements performed at multiple modulation frequencies, spot sizes, or delay-time windows can provide ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "complementary sensitivity signatures and improve parameter separability relative to a single experimental condition. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "The accessible range of thermal scales nevertheless remains constrained by the laser repetition rate, mechanical-delay ")
]

// arXiv source page 20
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "20 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "range, heat accumulation, modulation and detection bandwidths, and signal-to-noise ratio. TDTR should therefore be ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "viewed as combining ultrafast delay-resolved sampling with additional experimental variables that tune the spatial and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "periodic characteristics of the thermal field, rather than as possessing a single fixed probing depth. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 115.1pt, "SPS combines frequency-dependent control of the thermal-diffusion scale with intra-period temporal information. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "Through square-wave modulation and acquisition of the full-period temperature waveform, SPS not only changes the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "characteristic heat-diffusion length with modulation frequency but also retains the thermal evolution during different ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "portions of the heating and cooling cycle. Across an appropriate frequency range, the relative contributions of the near-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "surface region, buried structure, substrate, and overall thermal-resistance network can therefore vary substantially, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "while different portions of the waveform can provide additional and potentially distinct sensitivity signatures. This ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "combination allows multifrequency, full-period measurements to place multiple constraints on parameter estimation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "within a common experimental configuration. As with FDTR, however, neither a particular modulation frequency nor ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "a particular portion of the waveform corresponds uniquely to an individual layer or thermophysical property. The ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "effective sensitivity range must therefore be established from the multilayer geometry, characteristic diffusion scales, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "and sensitivity analysis. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 286.73pt, "Unlike the preceding approaches, which primarily manipulate or sample heat diffusion through temporal and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "frequency-related variables, SDTR and related spatial-scanning methods introduce lateral position as an explicit ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "measurement coordinate. Coordinated selection of modulation frequency, laser-spot size, and spatial scan range can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "match the characteristic lateral diffusion length to the optical geometry and thereby enhance sensitivity to in-plane heat ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "transport. When the lateral diffusion length is much smaller than the spot size, the measured spatial profile is influenced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "more strongly by the local optical intensity distribution and localized cross-plane heat flow, reducing sensitivity to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "intrinsic in-plane transport. When the thermal and optical length scales become comparable, lateral spreading produces ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "a more pronounced modification of the measured spatial profile, increasing sensitivity to in-plane thermal properties. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "The distinguishing feature of spatial-domain methods is therefore not simply access to a larger diffusion length, but ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "direct measurement of the lateral evolution of the thermal field, extending characteristic-scale analysis from cross-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "penetration to in-plane thermal spreading. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 458.35pt, "In summary, the representative thermoreflectance techniques provide different experimental routes for selecting ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "or interrogating characteristic heat-diffusion scales. SSTR probes the long-time, quasi-steady thermal field; TTR ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "samples transient diffusion through the observation-time window; FDTR tunes the periodic thermal penetration depth ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "through modulation frequency; TDTR resolves ultrafast thermal evolution through pump–probe delay while using ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "modulation frequency, spot size, and fitting window to redistribute sensitivity; SPS combines frequency-dependent ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "diffusion scales with full-period temporal information; and SDTR directly resolves lateral thermal spreading through ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "spatial scanning while using modulation frequency and spot size to control the relevant in-plane diffusion scale. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "Accordingly, no thermoreflectance technique possesses a universal measurement depth or fixed applicable length scale. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "Experimental conditions should instead be selected by matching the characteristic thermal-diffusion lengths to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "sample dimensions and by verifying, through sensitivity analysis, that the resulting observable contains sufficient and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "distinguishable information about the target thermophysical properties. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.7pt, "3.2 Parameter Sensitivity and Identifiability ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 668.98pt, "The definitions of parameter sensitivity and correlation, together with the distinction between a parameter being ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "detectable in the measured response and independently identifiable from other unknowns, were introduced in Section ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "1.4. Rather than repeating these concepts, this section compares how the different measurement dimensions available ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "to thermoreflectance techniques generate distinct sensitivity signatures and thereby influence parameter identifiability. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 731.38pt, "From a comparative perspective, SSTR and TTR rely primarily on a quasi-steady response or a single transient ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "decay, respectively, and are therefore most effective for systems with relatively few unknown parameters or with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "sufficient prior information to constrain the inverse problem. FDTR acquires frequency-dependent thermal responses ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "through modulation-frequency scanning and can exploit the resulting redistribution of parameter sensitivities across ")
]

// arXiv source page 21
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "21 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "frequency to constrain film, interface, and substrate properties. Its ability to separate multiple unknowns depends not ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "only on the magnitude of sensitivity to each parameter, but more importantly on whether their frequency-dependent ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "sensitivity signatures are sufficiently distinct over the experimentally accessible range. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 115.1pt, "TDTR uses pump–probe delay time as its primary measurement coordinate and can generate complementary ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "sensitivity conditions by varying modulation frequency, laser-spot size, and the selected fitting window. SPS retains ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "the complete intra-period temperature waveform at different modulation frequencies, thereby providing both frequency-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "dependent and intra-period temporal sensitivity information. These approaches can improve parameter identifiability ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "when the additional experimental conditions produce sufficiently different sensitivity signatures for the target ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "unknowns. An increased number of measured data points alone, however, does not increase the effective dimensionality ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "of the inverse problem if those data carry largely redundant parameter information. Strongly correlated parameters can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "therefore remain difficult to estimate independently even from large datasets. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 239.9pt, "SDTR and related spatial-scanning or beam-offset methods introduce lateral spatial information, which is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "particularly valuable for distinguishing sensitivity to in-plane heat transport from that associated with cross-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "diffusion and interfaces. In anisotropic or multilayer systems, spatial measurements can be combined with frequency- ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "or time-domain information to generate complementary sensitivity signatures and modify the correlation structure ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "among in-plane thermal conductivity, cross-plane thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and other ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "unknown parameters. The benefit of introducing a spatial dimension therefore depends on whether the spatial response ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "provides information that is sufficiently distinct from that already contained in the temporal or frequency-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "observables. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 364.73pt, "The central issue in a multiparameter inverse problem is therefore not to identify a single universally superior ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "thermoreflectance technique, but to determine whether the available observations provide sufficiently distinct ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "information to resolve the target parameter set with acceptable uncertainty. This condition can be evaluated using a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "sensitivity matrix and related tools such as the Fisher information matrix, singular-value decomposition, parameter-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "correlation metrics, or posterior-correlation analysis. Poorly conditioned sensitivity matrices or nearly collinear ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "sensitivity vectors indicate that different parameter combinations can produce similar changes in the measured response, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "even when the individual parameter sensitivities are large. Additional modulation frequencies, delay-time windows, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "spot sizes, spatial offsets, or independently constrained parameters can then be introduced to improve the conditioning ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "of the inverse problem rather than merely increase the number of observations. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 505.15pt, "For complex systems, diversity of parameter sensitivity across complementary measurement dimensions is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "generally more valuable than a larger number of highly redundant data points acquired under nearly identical conditions. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "Experimental design should therefore begin with the target parameter set and sample geometry and then select ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "measurement coordinates and control variables that maximize useful sensitivity while minimizing parameter correlation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "within realistic experimental constraints. Available prior information and expected measurement uncertainty should ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "also be incorporated into this assessment. Identifiability should ultimately be evaluated both before measurement, to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "guide experimental design, and after inversion, to quantify parameter covariance and determine whether the extracted ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "properties are uniquely constrained by the available data. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.7pt, "3.3 Capability for Distinguishing In-Plane and Cross-Plane Heat Transport ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 668.98pt, "For thin films, layered structures, and anisotropic materials, in-plane and cross-plane thermal conductivities can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "differ substantially, making directional sensitivity and identifiability important considerations in method selection. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "From the perspective of characteristic length scales, when the lateral thermal-diffusion length is small relative to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "laser-spot size, heat flow beneath the heated region approaches a predominantly cross-plane regime, and the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "response is generally more sensitive to cross-plane thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "associated cross-plane thermal resistances. As lateral thermal spreading becomes appreciable relative to the optical-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "spot size or pump–probe offset, radial or directional in-plane heat flow contributes more strongly to the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "response, thereby increasing sensitivity to in-plane thermal conductivity. ")
]

// arXiv source page 22
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "22 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 68.28pt, "In conventional coaxial pump–probe configurations, TTR, FDTR, and TDTR often operate under conditions ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "designed to emphasize cross-plane heat transport. FDTR uses modulation frequency to tune the characteristic thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "penetration depth, while TDTR additionally exploits pump–probe delay time and modulation frequency to redistribute ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "sensitivity among quantities such as cross-plane thermal conductivity and interfacial thermal conductance. Decreasing ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "the laser-spot size or modulation frequency can increase the relative contribution of radial heat spreading and thereby ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "introduce sensitivity to in-plane thermal conductivity. However, an axisymmetric coaxial geometry primarily probes ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "an azimuthally averaged lateral response and therefore provides limited information for distinguishing thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "conductivities along different in-plane directions, particularly in strongly anisotropic materials. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 193.1pt, "When the target parameters include in-plane thermal conductivity or in-plane anisotropy, introducing a lateral ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "pump–probe displacement or spatial-scanning coordinate can substantially redistribute parameter sensitivity toward ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "lateral heat transport. SDTR and BO-TDTR explicitly use spatially resolved measurements, while fixed-offset ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "implementations such as BO-FDTR and BO-SPS modify the heat-flow geometry to enhance sensitivity to in-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "transport [66, 68, 69]. For anisotropic materials, spatial measurements performed along different in-plane directions ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "and interpreted using an anisotropic heat-diffusion model can provide direction-dependent thermal-conductivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "information and, with sufficient directional measurements, constrain the in-plane conductivity tensor. Introducing a ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "spatial dimension or beam offset does not, however, isolate in-plane transport from cross-plane conductivity, interfacial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "thermal conductance, or substrate properties. The modulation frequency, pump and probe spot sizes, offset or scan ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "range, and sample geometry must therefore be jointly optimized so that the resulting sensitivity signatures provide ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "adequate directional discrimination while limiting parameter correlation. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 364.73pt, "Overall, coaxial TDTR, FDTR, and SPS can be configured to provide strong sensitivity to cross-plane film thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "conductivity, interfacial thermal conductance, and multilayer thermal resistance, whereas spatial-scanning and beam-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "offset configurations provide additional leverage for characterizing in-plane transport and anisotropy. The distinction ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "is not absolute: coaxial measurements can retain appreciable sensitivity to in-plane transport when lateral heat spreading ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "is significant, while spatially resolved measurements remain coupled to cross-plane transport and interfaces. For ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "complex anisotropic systems in which multiple quantities such as ")
