  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "multiple modulation frequencies or spot sizes, SPS, or joint analysis of complementary measurement configurations or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "techniques. SPS provides one such route by combining frequency-dependent control of the heat-diffusion scale with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "full-period temporal information. Its practical benefit in a given system, however, depends on whether the additional ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "waveform and frequency information produces sufficiently distinct parameter sensitivities, as well as on low-frequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "stability, waveform fidelity, available prior information, and experimental signal-to-noise ratio. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 146.3pt, "For two-dimensional materials, oriented films, and anisotropic crystals, the measurement configuration generally ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "needs to enhance or explicitly resolve lateral heat diffusion when in-plane thermal properties are the quantities of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "interest. SDTR and BO-TDTR introduce spatially resolved information through lateral scanning, whereas fixed-offset ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "implementations such as BO-FDTR and BO-SPS modify the heat-flow geometry to increase sensitivity to in-plane ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "transport. Measurements performed along different in-plane directions can further provide direction-dependent ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "sensitivity to thermal-conductivity anisotropy. Nevertheless, cross-plane thermal conductivity, interfacial thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "conductance, substrate properties, and optical-beam geometry can remain coupled to the measured response. Reliable ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "multidirectional or three-dimensional thermal characterization may therefore require measurements at multiple spatial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "directions, frequencies, spot sizes, or offsets, together with appropriate prior constraints and anisotropic heat-diffusion ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "modeling. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 302.33pt, "The purpose of Table 1 is therefore not to establish a performance hierarchy among thermoreflectance techniques, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "but to provide qualitative guidance for matching experimental methods and conditions to specific thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "characterization problems. Method selection should account for film thickness, thermal-conductivity range, interface ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "depth, thermal anisotropy, relevant thermal-diffusion length scales, instrument bandwidth, and the availability and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "uncertainty of independently known parameters. Sensitivity and uncertainty analyses should then be used to determine ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "whether the target properties can be resolved with sufficient identifiability and acceptable uncertainty under the selected ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "conditions. Accordingly, the measurement scales and application categories summarized in Table 1 should be ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "interpreted as representative operating regimes rather than fixed applicability boundaries; substantial overlap among ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "techniques is expected, and the appropriate method ultimately depends on the combined sample geometry, target ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "parameter set, and experimental configuration. ")
