  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "thermal diffusivity of the material, while the effective region contributing to the measured response is further governed ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "by the multilayer architecture, optical geometry, and experimental operating conditions. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 99.5pt, "Within the submicrometer-to-tens-of-micrometers characteristic length-scale regime, high-frequency TDTR, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "FDTR, and SPS can weight the thermal response more strongly toward the transducer, near-surface layers, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "interfaces and are therefore commonly applied to measurements of cross-plane film thermal conductivity and interfacial ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "thermal conductance. As modulation frequency decreases or the relevant observation time increases, the characteristic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "thermal-diffusion length increases, and the measured response becomes progressively more sensitive to buried layers, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "the substrate, and longer-range thermal transport. Low-frequency FDTR and SPS, together with TTR measurements ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "over appropriate transient time windows, can therefore access larger heat-diffusion scales relevant to thicker films, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "multilayer structures, and bulk materials. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 224.3pt, "When the characteristic lateral thermal-diffusion length becomes comparable to the laser-spot size or pump–probe ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "offset, lateral heat spreading contributes more strongly to the measured temperature field. By introducing lateral ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "position or beam offset into the measurement geometry, SDTR and related beam-offset methods can enhance sensitivity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "to in-plane thermal transport and are therefore particularly useful for characterizing in-plane thermal conductivity and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "thermal anisotropy. At sufficiently long timescales or low modulation frequencies, the thermal response approaches the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "quasi-steady limit. SSTR directly exploits this regime, while sufficiently low-frequency periodic methods can approach ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "quasi-steady behavior during portions of the excitation cycle, increasing sensitivity to the combined thermal resistance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "and longer-range heat spreading of the sample system. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 349.13pt, "The principal purpose of Fig. 4 is therefore to illustrate the continuity and substantial overlap among the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "characteristic heat-diffusion scales accessible to different thermoreflectance techniques, rather than to define absolute ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "applicability boundaries. No single diffusion-length range uniquely corresponds to a particular technique or ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "thermophysical property, and the appropriate experimental scheme should instead be selected according to film ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "thickness, interface depth, thermal diffusivity, optical-spot size, sample architecture, and the target thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "parameters. For polymers, porous materials, and other systems with thermal diffusivities substantially lower than that ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "of single-crystal silicon, the characteristic thermal-diffusion length at a given modulation frequency or observation time ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "is correspondingly smaller. Accessing a comparable cross-plane diffusion length therefore generally requires a lower ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "modulation frequency or a longer observation timescale, whereas adjustment of the laser-spot size can be used to control ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "the relative importance of lateral and cross-plane heat flow. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 521.23pt, "4 ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 521.23pt, "Method Matching and Case Studies for Representative Complex Measurement Scenarios ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 544.15pt, "The preceding sections compared thermoreflectance techniques in terms of heat-diffusion scale, parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "identifiability, and in-plane/cross-plane heat transport. This section examines four complex measurement problems and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "uses representative studies to show how the independent information required by a problem determines the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "experimental scheme. Several figures in this section are drawn from recent SPS and BO-SPS studies because they ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "provide clear examples of how broadband frequency, periodic-waveform, and spatial-offset degrees of freedom can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "constrain parameters; their inclusion does not imply that SPS is generally superior to TDTR, FDTR, SDTR, or other ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "methods in these scenarios. For a specific sample, the most appropriate scheme remains dependent on instrument ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "bandwidth, sample dimensions, available prior information, and target uncertainty. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.7pt, "4.1 Isotropic Low-Thermal-Conductivity Materials: Joint Estimation of Thermal Conductivity and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.3pt, "Volumetric Heat Capacity ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 723.58pt, "Thermoreflectance signals are primarily sensitive to two combinations of sample properties: the in-plane thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.78pt, "diffusivity (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(98.904pt, 739.78pt, "𝛼= 𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(125.06pt, 741.96pt, "𝑟")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(129.74pt, 739.78pt, "/𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(141.5pt, 739.78pt, ") and a cross-plane thermal-effusivity-related quantity (")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(372.07pt, 739.78pt, "𝑒= √𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(404.95pt, 741.96pt, "𝑧")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(409.51pt, 739.78pt, "𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(416.11pt, 739.78pt, "). For a homogeneous isotropic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "material, the in-plane and cross-plane thermal conductivities satisfy ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(339.31pt, 754.8pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(345.19pt, 756.96pt, "𝑟")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(352.63pt, 754.8pt, "= 𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(369.31pt, 756.96pt, "𝑧")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(376.63pt, 754.8pt, "= 𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(393.55pt, 754.8pt, ". If complementary experiments can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 770.4pt, "determine ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(96.024pt, 770.4pt, "𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(102.62pt, 770.4pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(126.5pt, 770.4pt, "𝑒")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(131.9pt, 770.4pt, " independently, thermal conductivity and volumetric heat capacity can then be obtained jointly ")
]

