#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, (image("graphics/page-26.png", width: 77pt), 222.5pt, 59.5pt))[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "26 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.88pt, "through the analytical relations ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(190.73pt, 68.88pt, "𝑘= 𝑒√𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(229.49pt, 68.88pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(254.57pt, 68.88pt, "𝐶= 𝑒/√𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(299.11pt, 68.88pt, " . Thus, simultaneous characterization of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(480.34pt, 68.88pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(486.46pt, 68.88pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(511.54pt, 68.88pt, "𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(518.26pt, 68.88pt, " in an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "isotropic material is not simply a matter of adding another unknown fitting parameter; it requires experimental ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "conditions under which the signal exhibits distinguishable sensitivities to ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(366.19pt, 99.5pt, "𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(372.79pt, 99.5pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(396.31pt, 99.5pt, "𝑒")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(401.71pt, 99.5pt, " at different heat-diffusion scales, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "thereby breaking the parameter correlation. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 130.7pt, "TDTR and FDTR have established a mature technical foundation for such two-parameter estimation. Variable-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "frequency TDTR continuously adjusts the pump modulation frequency to change thermal penetration depth and has ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "been used to measure ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(147.5pt, 161.9pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(153.62pt, 161.9pt, " and ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(177.29pt, 161.9pt, "𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(184.01pt, 161.9pt, " simultaneously in bulk and thin-film materials [71]. Dual-frequency TDTR further ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "exploits differences in the thermal response at two modulation frequencies to perform volumetric-heat-capacity imaging ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "with micrometer-scale spatial resolution [72]. In the frequency domain, the FDTR method introduced by Schmidt et al. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "acquires amplitude and phase as functions of modulation frequency, providing an independent frequency-control ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "dimension for estimating thermal conductivity, thermal diffusivity, and volumetric-heat-capacity-related properties in ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "homogeneous bulk and thin-film systems [10]. Subsequent broadband FDTR studies further demonstrated simultaneous ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "extraction of thermal conductivity and volumetric heat capacity in isotropic materials [73]. More recently, variants ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "including negative-delay TDTR, variable-spot-size TDTR, and beam-offset TDTR/FDTR [53, 69, 74, 75] have ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "expanded the multiparameter-decoupling degrees of freedom of TDTR/FDTR through the delay-time window, beam ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "geometry, and lateral spatial control, respectively. These studies show that conventional time- and frequency-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "thermoreflectance is not limited to single-parameter thermal-conductivity measurement; with coordinated design of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "multiple experimental variables, joint estimation of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(269.09pt, 333.53pt, "𝑘")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(275.21pt, 333.53pt, ", ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(282.05pt, 333.53pt, "𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(288.77pt, 333.53pt, ", and interfacial thermal conductance is possible. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 349.13pt, "For an isotropic low-thermal-conductivity polymer such as poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA), however, the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "principal challenge in joint multiparameter estimation is whether the experimental system can reach sufficiently low ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 380.33pt, "modulation frequencies to establish a long heat-diffusion length scale. The thermal diffusivity of PMMA is on the order ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 396.05pt, "of ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(62.544pt, 396.05pt, "10")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(74.304pt, 392.21pt, "−7")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(84.504pt, 396.05pt, " m")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 7.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(95.544pt, 392.21pt, "2")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(100.34pt, 396.05pt, "/s")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(109.94pt, 396.05pt, ". With a laser spot on the order of tens of micrometers, the pump modulation frequency must be reduced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "to the hundreds-of-hertz range or below for the lateral diffusion length to become comparable to the spot radius and for ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "in-plane heat transport to contribute appreciably to the signal. Conventional TDTR provides excellent temporal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "resolution and mature multilayer thermal models, but its commonly used modulation frequencies are in the MHz range. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "Constraints associated with the intrinsic laser repetition rate, long-time pulse accumulation, and the stability of low-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "frequency lock-in detection make it difficult for a conventional TDTR configuration to access directly the ultralow-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "frequency in-plane diffusion regime required for such polymers. FDTR can increase penetration depth by lowering ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "modulation frequency, but low-frequency measurements generally require much longer integration times and become ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "more susceptible to environmental thermal drift, phase-calibration error, and reduced signal-to-noise ratio. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "Consequently, when conventional TDTR or FDTR is used alone for low-thermal-conductivity isotropic materials, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 551.95pt, "parameter coupling and low-frequency limitations often make it necessary to prescribe a literature value of volumetric ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "heat capacity or determine heat capacity independently before thermal conductivity can be estimated stably. These are ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "not absolute limits of TDTR or FDTR: lowering modulation frequency, changing spot size, introducing independent ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "heat-capacity information, or jointly fitting multiple conditions can all provide workable solutions, depending on ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "instrument performance and target uncertainty. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 629.98pt, "For this low-thermal-conductivity case, SPS provides one implementation route for building complementary ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "constraints from a broad frequency range and complete periodic waveforms. A square-wave-modulated heat source is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "used and the full temperature waveform is recorded, allowing the diffusion length to be varied continuously from hertz ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "to megahertz frequencies. Low-frequency waveforms can capture fully developed in-plane diffusion in a low-thermal-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "conductivity material, while high-frequency waveforms provide constraints related to near-surface cross-plane thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 707.98pt, "effusivity. Compared with approaches such as negative-delay TDTR that mainly extend the use of existing time-domain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "information, this SPS implementation extends the accessible experimental conditions to lower modulation frequencies ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "and thereby supplies complementary information for decoupling ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(324.43pt, 739.18pt, "𝛼")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(331.03pt, 739.18pt, ", ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(337.87pt, 739.18pt, "𝑒")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(343.27pt, 739.18pt, ", and ultimately ")
  #set text(font: "Cambria Math", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(413.11pt, 739.18pt, "𝑘−𝐶")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(438.46pt, 739.18pt, ". ")
]

