#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "35 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "particular technique first, but to identify which thermophysical properties must be distinguished and then determine ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "what mutually independent temporal, frequency, spatial, or waveform information the available experiment can provide. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "The frequent use of SPS and BO-SPS examples in this section reflects the availability of representative recent studies ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "and figures, not a ranking of method performance. For the same scientific problem, multifrequency TDTR/FDTR, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "variable-spot or beam-offset measurements, SPS, SDTR, and cross-platform combinations can all be viable. The final ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "choice should be evaluated on parameter identifiability, measurement uncertainty, experimental complexity, and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "reproducibility. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 12pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.58pt, "5 ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 193.58pt, "Challenges, Trends, and Outlook ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.42pt, "5.1 Ill-Posed Inversion and Uncertainty Quantification ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 247.73pt, "Sections 1.4 and 3.2 discussed identifiability in thermoreflectance inversion from the perspectives of local ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 263.33pt, "sensitivity and parameter correlation. For multilayer heterostructures, anisotropic materials, and complex devices, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 278.93pt, "however, local sensitivity analysis alone is not sufficient to establish the uniqueness or credibility of the inferred ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 294.53pt, "parameters. Even when the experimental signal is reproduced well by a heat-diffusion model, the objective function ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "may contain multiple parameter combinations that are statistically or practically indistinguishable within the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 325.73pt, "experimental uncertainty. Strong parameter correlations, dependence on initial guesses, and uncertainties in nominally ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "fixed inputs can further affect the inferred results. The central challenge in complex inverse problems is therefore not ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "simply to obtain a satisfactory fit, but to determine which parameters or parameter combinations are actually ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "constrained by the available data and to quantify the uncertainty associated with those estimates. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 388.13pt, "Error propagation and Monte Carlo sampling are widely used to propagate experimental and input uncertainties ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "into inferred thermophysical properties, while Bayesian inversion provides a probabilistic framework for ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "multiparameter inference. Bayesian approaches combine prior information, experimental observations, and an assumed ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "measurement-error model to construct posterior distributions that describe parameter uncertainty and correlation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "conditioned on the adopted physical and statistical models [91]. Compared with reporting only a best-fit value and an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "associated standard uncertainty, posterior distributions can more directly reveal poorly constrained parameters, non-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "Gaussian uncertainty, multimodality, and correlations among inferred quantities. Such approaches shift ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "thermoreflectance analysis from point estimation toward uncertainty-aware parameter inference, although the resulting ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "posterior remains dependent on the assumed priors, likelihood, and forward-model fidelity. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 528.55pt, "Parameter uncertainty alone, however, does not fully characterize the credibility of a thermoreflectance ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "measurement. Most inversion procedures assume that the selected heat-diffusion model adequately represents the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "underlying transport physics. When the relevant thermal length and time scales approach regimes in which Fourier ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "diffusion becomes inadequate, or when complex interfaces, nonequilibrium processes, or other unmodeled physics ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "become important, the forward model itself can introduce systematic bias. Under such conditions, a small fitting ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "residual may simply indicate that adjustable parameters compensate for model inadequacy rather than that the inferred ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "properties accurately represent the physical system. Uncertainty analysis for complex systems should therefore ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "distinguish uncertainty in fitted parameters and experimental inputs from uncertainty or discrepancy associated with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "the forward model. The applicable regime of the model should also be assessed explicitly so that fitted thermophysical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 668.98pt, "parameters are not inadvertently used to absorb deficiencies in the assumed transport description. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 684.58pt, "Uncertainty quantification can further be incorporated directly into experimental design rather than being applied ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "only after parameter fitting. Conventional measurements often select modulation frequencies, delay-time windows, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "laser-spot sizes, or spatial offsets in advance and subsequently invert the resulting data. In strongly coupled systems, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "however, collecting additional observations under conditions with similar sensitivity signatures may provide little new ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "information about the unknown parameters. Sensitivity matrices, parameter-correlation measures, and the Fisher ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "information matrix can be used before measurement to evaluate the expected information content of candidate ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "experimental conditions and identify configurations that improve parameter separability [92]. After initial ")
]

