#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "36 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "measurements are acquired, subsequent conditions can in principle be selected according to the updated parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "uncertainty or expected information gain, establishing a closed loop among experimental design, data acquisition, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "parameter inference, and uncertainty updating. Such adaptive strategies seek not merely to increase the quantity of data, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "but to acquire measurements that most effectively reduce uncertainty or distinguish among competing parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "combinations. This framework also provides a natural foundation for incorporating fast surrogate models and machine-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "learning-assisted optimization into thermoreflectance experiments. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 161.9pt, "Future thermoreflectance data analysis is therefore likely to place increasing emphasis on credible inference, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "model adequacy, and information-efficient experimental design, rather than on fitting speed or residual minimization ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "alone. Combining probabilistic inversion, model-discrepancy assessment, computationally efficient surrogate models, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 208.7pt, "and adaptive experimental design could enable measurement conditions to be selected dynamically according to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "information already acquired. Such a closed-loop framework can target experimental conditions that reduce parameter ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "correlation, constrain the most uncertain quantities, and avoid redundant measurements. For complex materials and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "devices, these developments could move thermoreflectance beyond conventional offline parameter fitting toward ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "adaptive, uncertainty-aware characterization in which measurement and inference are jointly optimized according to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "parameter identifiability and information gain. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 318.05pt, "5.2 Scale Effects and Model Boundaries in Extending Spatiotemporal Resolution ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 341.33pt, "The effective spatiotemporal resolution of thermoreflectance measurements is jointly constrained by multiple ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "characteristic scales, including thermal penetration depth, phonon mean free path, laser-spot size, and metal-transducer ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "thickness. Changes in modulation frequency, spot size, and detection scheme affect not only the spatial and temporal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "scales sampled by the experiment but also the relative importance of different heat-transport processes in the measured ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "response. Further improvement in spatiotemporal resolution therefore cannot be understood simply as extending ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "measurements to higher frequencies or smaller optical spots. Instrumental resolution, the physical origin of the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "measured signal, characteristic transport length and time scales, and the validity of the adopted forward model must ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "instead be considered together. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 466.15pt, "At high modulation frequencies, the thermal penetration depth decreases and can become comparable to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 481.75pt, "thickness of an Al or Au transducer. Under such conditions, an increasing fraction of the temperature response is ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "localized within the transducer and its immediate thermal environment, and the measured signal can become more ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "sensitive to transducer heat capacity and metal/sample interfacial thermal conductance while exhibiting reduced ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "sensitivity to the intrinsic thermophysical properties of the underlying material [94]. Reducing the transducer thickness ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "can partially alleviate this limitation, but may also alter optical absorption and thermoreflectance sensitivity and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "increase the relative importance of uncertainties in film thickness, continuity, morphology, and interface quality. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "Transducerless thermoreflectance provides an alternative by exploiting the material's intrinsic optical or electronic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "response for direct excitation and/or detection [95–98], thereby avoiding thermal and interfacial contributions ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "introduced by a deposited metal film. This approach, however, places more stringent requirements on the optical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "response, surface condition, and signal stability of the material itself. The transducer should therefore be regarded not ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "merely as an optical component but as part of the thermal measurement system whose dimensions and properties can ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "ultimately constrain the accessible heat-diffusion scale. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 668.98pt, "As the characteristic thermal length scales—such as the thermal penetration depth or laser-spot size—become ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "comparable to relevant phonon mean free paths, Fourier's local diffusive description can progressively lose validity, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "and quasiballistic or more generally nondiffusive transport can contribute appreciably to the measured response. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "Because heat in real materials is carried by phonons spanning broad distributions of mean free paths and relaxation ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "times, this transition generally occurs over a range of experimental length and time scales rather than at a single sharply ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "defined boundary. Under such conditions, an effective or apparent thermal conductivity inferred using a purely diffusive ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "model can exhibit systematic dependence on modulation frequency, spot size, or other experimental length scales [99–")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "101]. TDTR and FDTR have been used to investigate phonon mean-free-path-dependent transport and to infer ")
]

// arXiv source page 37
#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "37 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "cumulative contributions to thermal conductivity [8, 102, 103]. Such inverse problems, however, are intrinsically ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "model-dependent and can require assumptions regarding phonon dispersion, relaxation-time distributions, or the form ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "of the suppression function relating quasiballistic transport to the measured response. In multilayer heterostructures, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "spectral mismatch, mode-dependent transmission, and interfacial scattering can occur simultaneously, making intrinsic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 130.7pt, "bulk phonon transport and interface-specific transmission difficult to disentangle from thermoreflectance data alone ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.3pt, "[104]. Further investigation of nonequilibrium interfacial transport will therefore require closer integration of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 161.9pt, "experimental inversion with spectrally resolved or nongray Boltzmann transport models and microscopic calculations ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 177.5pt, "of mode-dependent interfacial transport [105], rather than representing complex interface effects solely through an ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 193.1pt, "effective bulk mean free path or a single phenomenological parameter. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 208.7pt, "At the low-frequency end, the dominant limitations increasingly arise from measurement stability, acquisition time, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 224.3pt, "and low-frequency noise. As the modulation period becomes longer, ambient-temperature drift, laser-power ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 239.9pt, "fluctuations, sample-stage instability, and other low-frequency disturbances can become increasingly important relative ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 255.53pt, "to the thermal signal. For low-frequency or steady-state absolute thermal-conductivity measurements of bulk materials, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 271.13pt, "established approaches such as the (3\\omega) method and steady-state calorimetry can offer complementary advantages ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 286.73pt, "in measurement stability and absolute-property determination [106, 107]. Thermoreflectance measurements at long ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 302.33pt, "timescales are therefore particularly valuable when their intrinsic advantages—noncontact optical interrogation, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 317.93pt, "localized heating and detection, microscale spatial selectivity, and tunable heat-flow geometry—are important to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 333.53pt, "measurement problem. Rather than requiring a single thermoreflectance platform to span all relevant timescales, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 349.13pt, "complementary use of optical, electrical, and calorimetric techniques can provide more robust coverage across different ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 364.73pt, "heat-diffusion regimes. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 380.33pt, "Further improvement in spatial resolution is ultimately constrained by the far-field optical diffraction limit. Near-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 395.93pt, "field optical approaches provide a possible route to nanoscale optical interrogation beyond conventional diffraction-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 411.55pt, "limited spot sizes [108]. Once excitation or detection occurs in the optical near field, however, localized electromagnetic ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 427.15pt, "fields, evanescent coupling, and the local electromagnetic density of states can influence energy deposition and signal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 442.75pt, "formation [109]. The measured thermoreflectance response may then depend not only on heat diffusion but also on ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 458.35pt, "probe geometry, probe–sample separation, material dielectric functions, and electromagnetic boundary conditions. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 473.95pt, "Consequently, interpreting a near-field optical signal as a local temperature measurement requires careful separation of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 489.55pt, "thermal effects from changes in the local optical interaction itself. Reliable extraction of temperature and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 505.15pt, "thermophysical properties in this regime will therefore require coupled electromagnetic–thermal forward models, ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 520.75pt, "together with quantitative assessment of probe perturbation, spatial averaging, and local optical-response effects on the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 536.35pt, "inverse problem. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 551.95pt, "As measurement dimensions continue to shrink and thermoreflectance is increasingly applied to operating devices ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 567.55pt, "and geometrically complex structures, sample geometry itself becomes an important constraint on model validity. Heat-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 583.18pt, "flow paths in curved structures, nonuniform films, micro- and nanodevices, and flexible electronics are governed jointly ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 598.78pt, "by local geometry, heterogeneous material distributions, heat-source dimensions, and boundary conditions and may no ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 614.38pt, "longer be represented adequately by conventional one-dimensional or axisymmetric multilayer models. Coupling finite-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 629.98pt, "element or other numerical forward models with parameter inversion allows experiment-specific geometry, spatially ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 645.58pt, "varying material properties, localized heat sources, and realistic boundary conditions to be incorporated directly into ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 661.18pt, "the forward problem [110]. Such numerical inversion frameworks can extend thermoreflectance beyond idealized ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 676.78pt, "planar structures toward complex engineering devices, although their increased number of geometric and material ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 692.38pt, "inputs also makes uncertainty propagation, model validation, and computational efficiency increasingly important. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 707.98pt, "Overall, further improvement in thermoreflectance spatiotemporal resolution will increasingly depend on co-")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 723.58pt, "designing experimental resolution with transport modeling and inversion fidelity. The objective should therefore not be ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 739.18pt, "higher modulation frequency, shorter timescale, or smaller optical spots alone, but the development of multiscale ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 754.8pt, "forward and inverse frameworks whose physical description remains appropriate as the relevant transport regime ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 770.4pt, "changes. Such frameworks may need to incorporate transducer effects, nondiffusive phonon transport, near-field ")
]

