#original-page(595.32pt, 841.92pt, none)[
  #set text(font: "DengXian", size: 9pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(292.85pt, 803.64pt, "38 ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 68.28pt, "electromagnetic coupling, and complex device geometry, while quantifying the uncertainty and model discrepancy ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 83.9pt, "introduced at each scale. Only when increased experimental resolution is accompanied by commensurate model fidelity ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 99.5pt, "and parameter identifiability can thermoreflectance measurements be interpreted reliably in regimes involving ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 115.1pt, "nonequilibrium interfacial transport and highly localized heat transfer in real micro- and nanodevices. ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 11.04pt, weight: "bold", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(49.56pt, 146.42pt, "5.3 Multiphysics Measurements Under Device Operating Conditions and Engineering Applications ")
  #set text(font: "Times New Roman", size: 10.56pt, weight: "regular", style: "normal", fill: rgb("#000000"))
  #line-at(70.584pt, 169.7pt, "As thermoreflectance moves from standard thin-film samples toward practical devices, measurements are ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 185.3pt, "increasingly extending from ex situ or near-equilibrium characterization to in situ and operando measurements under ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 200.9pt, "realistic operating conditions. Electrical excitation, transient self-heating, mechanical deformation, and other physical ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 216.5pt, "processes can occur simultaneously during device operation. Coupling among these processes can modify the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 232.1pt, "underlying heat-transport behavior while also introducing nonthermal or indirectly thermal contributions to the ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 247.73pt, "measured optical response. The central challenge for device-oriented thermoreflectance is therefore not simply to ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 263.33pt, "achieve higher spatiotemporal resolution, but to separate coupled physical contributions quantitatively while retaining ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 278.93pt, "the electrical, thermal, and mechanical conditions relevant to actual device operation. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 294.53pt, "In wide-bandgap power devices, transient self-heating is strongly coupled to carrier transport, and the local ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 310.13pt, "temperature field evolves dynamically with switching conditions, current distribution, and power dissipation. ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 325.73pt, "Measurements performed after interrupting or substantially perturbing the electrical bias may therefore fail to reproduce ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 341.33pt, "the thermal state present during active operation. Recently developed on-the-fly thermoreflectance synchronizes ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 356.93pt, "electrical excitation with optical probing to capture in situ temperature evolution within selected temporal windows ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 372.53pt, "during device transients [111]. The measured reflectance can also contain electrically modulated contributions ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 388.13pt, "associated with electrothermal and thermoelectric phenomena, including the Seebeck effect [112]. Distinguishing ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 403.73pt, "temperature-induced reflectance changes from reflectance variations associated with electrical excitation or other ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 419.35pt, "coupled mechanisms may provide access to complementary information on electrothermal transport, but requires ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 434.95pt, "appropriate modulation schemes, calibration, and physical modeling. Thermoreflectance is thus evolving from ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 450.55pt, "conventional temperature sensing toward operando characterization of coupled electrical and thermal behavior, with ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 466.15pt, "reliable separation and interpretation of the different signal contributions remaining a central challenge. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 481.75pt, "In flexible electronics, polymer-based devices, and advanced packaging, heat transport is also frequently coupled ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 497.35pt, "to mechanical deformation and evolving interface conditions. Thermal cycling, mechanical bending, or applied strain ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 512.95pt, "can alter interfacial contact, local microstructure, and material properties, thereby modifying interfacial thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 528.55pt, "conductance and lateral heat spreading. At the same time, thermal expansion and thermoelastic deformation can modify ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 544.15pt, "the optical response or provide an additional measurable observable. Photothermal deformation techniques exploit ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 559.75pt, "surface displacement or curvature changes to infer thermophysical or thermomechanical properties such as thermal ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 575.38pt, "conductivity and thermal-expansion coefficient [113–115]. Combining thermoreflectance-based temperature sensing ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 590.98pt, "with photothermal deformation measurements and controlled in situ mechanical loading could therefore provide ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 606.58pt, "complementary observables for tracking the coupled evolution of temperature, deformation, and interface state during ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 622.18pt, "strain or thermal cycling. Such measurements could provide a multiphysics framework for investigating ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 637.78pt, "thermomechanical degradation, interface evolution, and heat-transfer failure mechanisms in flexible devices and ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 653.38pt, "advanced packaging. ")
  #line-at(70.584pt, 668.98pt, "From an engineering perspective, extending thermoreflectance toward wafer-scale mapping, high-throughput ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 684.58pt, "screening, and in-line metrology will require simultaneous improvements in measurement throughput, process ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 700.18pt, "compatibility, automated data analysis, and uncertainty-controlled parameter extraction. Conventional TDTR ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 715.78pt, "commonly relies on mechanical delay-line scanning, whereas asynchronous optical sampling and other electronically ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 731.38pt, "or optically encoded time-domain acquisition schemes can reduce or eliminate mechanical delay scanning and thereby ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 747pt, "increase acquisition speed. For frequency- and waveform-domain approaches such as FDTR and SPS, multifrequency ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 762.6pt, "excitation, broadband acquisition, and digital demodulation provide potential routes to more efficient collection of ")
  #line-at(49.56pt, 778.2pt, "information-rich datasets. Metal transducers can introduce additional interfaces and may be incompatible with ")
]

